WEBVTT

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:07.630
<v Ira Glass>About a year and a half ago, when my mom was dying, I had this experience that I bring up here because I think it's so common.
<v Ira Glass>大约一年半前，我母亲弥留之际，我有了这样一种经历，之所以在这里提起，是因为我认为这太普遍了。

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:36.500
<v Ira Glass>She and I were in different cities, and I would go see her most weekends when she got sick. It was pretty clear she didn't have a lot of time left. And I kept thinking, we should be having these really big conversations. And I don't even know what it is that I wanted to talk about. It just seemed like anything that we needed to say to each other, we'd better say now before it was too late. But truthfully, it wasn't like we had big issues that needed to be resolving or anything like that. All that stuff had been done years ago.
<v Ira Glass>她和我住在不同的城市，她生病的时候，我几乎每个周末都会去看她。很明显她剩下的时间不多了。我一直在想，我们应该进行一些非常重要的对话。我甚至不知道我想聊些什么。只是觉得如果我们之间有什么话需要对彼此说，最好现在就说，免得为时已晚。但老实说，并不是我们之间有什么需要解决的重大问题之类的。所有那些事情多年前就已经处理好了。

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:57.020
<v Ira Glass>And so I would go. And mostly what she wanted was just company. So we'd watch old movies, and we'd watch TV, and we'd play a little cards and chat about whatever small thing would come up. And at one level, this was fine. It was completely fine. It was fine. And like I say, it was what she wanted.
<v Ira Glass>所以我去了。她想要的主要是陪伴。于是我们一起看老电影，看电视，玩玩纸牌，聊聊随口想到的琐事。从某种层面上讲，这很好。这完全没问题。很好。就像我说的，这正是她想要的。

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:24.370
<v Ira Glass>But at another level, I would sit there intermittently and secretly very anxious that I was wasting this time. That in 6 months or 2 years or 10 years, I was going to look back, and I was going to feel sorry that we had never talked about-- you know what? I didn't even know what we hadn't talked about. I don't know. Like who was her best friend in junior high school, or what her house was like when she was a little girl.
<v Ira Glass>但在另一个层面上，我会断断续续地坐在那儿，暗自非常焦虑，觉得我在浪费这段时间。我在想，6个月后、2年后或者10年后，回首往事，我会因为我们从未谈论过——你知道吗？我甚至不知道我们没谈论过什么。我不知道。比如她在初中时最好的朋友是谁，或者她小时候的家是什么样的。

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:44.330
<v Ira Glass>I have a friend whose father died not too long ago who told me, well, if nothing else, I should use this time to just ask all the questions about all those little stories that I think I might want to know some day. And we did talk about some of that stuff. There were some old stories that I never really had straight that now I know.
<v Ira Glass>我有一个朋友，他的父亲不久前去世了，他告诉我，就算别的什么都不做，我也应该利用这段时间问清楚那些我想在某天了解的琐碎往事。我们确实谈论了一些。有些我以前一直没弄明白的陈年旧事，现在我知道了。

00:01:44.330 --> 00:02:25.040
<v Ira Glass>And there were a couple of times sitting on the couch in the middle of some long afternoon where I asked my mom, is there anything that you want to talk about? Is anything that we need to talk about? Anything we need to have resolved or that we need to discuss? And she asked me, why, in my 20s, pretty much through my entire 20s, I was so distant from the family. And I asked her about all the years that she and my dad completely disapproved of my career in public radio. And we talked about these things, we talked about them, I would say, for like, I want to say a total of, I don't know, three minutes. And that pretty much exhausted it as subjects. And then we moved onto the next thing, and that was that.
<v Ira Glass>有几次我坐在沙发上，在漫长的下午中间，我问我妈妈，有什么你想聊的吗？有什么我们需要谈的吗？有什么需要解决或者需要讨论的事情吗？她问我，为什么我在20多岁的时候，几乎整个20多岁，都和家里那么疏远。我问她关于她和我父亲完全不赞同我从事公共广播事业的那几年。我们谈论了这些事情，我们谈论了它们，我会说，总共聊了，我不知道，三分钟吧。这些话题基本就聊完了。然后我们就转到了下一件事，就这样。

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:58.590
<v Ira Glass>Since she died, I've been waiting for the other shoe to drop. I keep waiting for the day that I'm going to be talking to somebody or reading something or watching TV, and suddenly I realize something that I want to ask my mom about. That suddenly I realize what we should have talked about during that stretch of all those weeks, what I should have said back then. I know it's going to happen, and the only question is, is it going to be tomorrow? Or is it going to be the next day? Or is it going to be next month? Or is it going to be next year?
<v Ira Glass>她去世后，我一直等着另一只靴子掉下来。我一直在等哪一天，我在和某人交谈，或者读到什么，或者看电视的时候，突然意识到有什么想问我妈妈的事情。突然意识到在那些周里我们本该谈论些什么，我当时本该说些什么。我知道这迟早会发生，唯一的问题是，会是明天吗？还是后天？还是下个月？还是明年？

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:23.660
<v Ira Glass>I know that people have this kind of feeling about the living also. You know what I mean? They have this feeling about their living friends and relatives, that they wish that they could go back and have do-overs and say something better or say the thing that it took them a really long time to figure out needed to be said. And in my experience-- maybe I'm unusual-- but in my experience, people almost never go back to do that. Even when the people are alive. They never go back and straighten it out and say it.
<v Ira Glass>我知道人们对活人也有这种感觉。你明白我的意思吗？他们对在世的亲友也有这种感觉，希望时光倒流，重来一次，说得更好一点，或者说出他们花了很长时间才想明白需要说的话。根据我的经验——也许我与众不同——但根据我的经验，人们几乎从不回头去那样做。即使对方还活着。他们从不回头去澄清并说出来。

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:40.060
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our radio program, we have stories about what it would mean to actually go back. What it would mean to finally say everything that you should have said at the time, but that most people never, ever do. We make an unscientific inquiry into that question.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，今天在我们的广播节目中，我们讲述了一些关于真正回头去做意味着什么的故事。最终说出你当时本该说出的话，而大多数人却从未做过，这意味着什么。我们对这个问题进行了一次非科学的调查。

00:03:40.060 --> 00:04:03.660
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Our program today in four time-traveling acts. Act One, Freeze Frame, in which Mr. Jonathan Goldstein, for once in his life, gets to suspend time itself, freeze the hands of time, and finally come up with the right thing to say in some situation.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ，这是《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们今天的节目分为四个穿越时空的片段。第一幕，定格，乔纳森·戈尔茨坦先生平生第一次能够暂停时间本身，冻结时间之手，并最终在某种情况下想出了正确的话来说。

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:39.180
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, In The Bush Leagues. We hear the true story of a 20-something activist who heckled the President of the United States, years ago, and failed horribly. President Bush completely bested this guy, leaving him mulling it over late at night for years. Act Three, A Can of Worms. David Sedaris wishes that he could take back a wish in an act. Act Four, Life Sentence, in which the president of the Maryland State Senate, a veteran politician, talks about the off-the-cuff remark that many people say changed his life forever.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，小联盟。我们听到了一个20多岁的活动家几年前质问美国总统，却惨败的真实故事。布什总统完全压制了这家伙，让他多年来在深夜里反复思量。第三幕，麻烦事。大卫·塞达里斯希望他能在某个举动中收回一个愿望。第四幕，终身监禁，马里兰州参议院院长，一位老资格的政治家，谈论了许多人所说的那句改变他一生的随口之言。

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:47.880
<v Ira Glass>Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>请继续收听。

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.780
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Freeze Frame.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，定格。

00:04:51.780 --> 00:05:11.810
<v Ira Glass>This is the sort of story that I think it's best to introduce the way that you would do a movie trailer. Ladies and gentlemen, if regret has a name-- a name, that is, besides regret-- that name is Jonathan Goldstein. Here he is, with some thoughts and a big plan to change things.
<v Ira Glass>我觉得最好用电影预告片的方式来介绍这个故事。女士们，先生们，如果遗憾有一个名字——一个名字，也就是说，除了遗憾之外——那个名字就是乔纳森·戈尔茨坦。他来了，带着一些想法和一个改变事情的大计划。

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:44.430
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Let me start by saying I have pretty much regretted every single thing that's ever come out of my mouth, from my bar mitzvah speech with its inordinate use of the word cherished, to the manner in which I recently responded to my dental hygienist when asked whether the office radio was on too loud. I said, no, it wasn't too loud. "In fact," I said, "it was totally cool." Lord knows where that came from. But once words are out there, they are out there, and there's nothing you can do about it.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>首先我要说，我几乎后悔我嘴里说出的每一句话，从我在受戒仪式上过度使用“珍惜”这个词的演讲，到最近我回答牙科保健师问我诊所广播是否太大声时的方式。我说，不，不大。“事实上，”我说，“这太酷了。”天知道那话是从哪儿来的。但一旦话出口了，就是出口了，你什么也做不了。

00:05:44.430 --> 00:06:05.990
<v Jonathan Goldstein>There are moments where I have misspoken or somehow failed to live up to the occasion that I am capable of replaying in my mind, searching for what I should have said, even years later. These moments have attached themselves to my brain like frightened cats that resist prying. And my capacity for regretting them sometimes feels like the deepest, most enduring part of my personality.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>有些时刻我失言了，或者未能达到我本能达到的场合，我会在脑海中重演，寻找我本该说的话，即使是多年后。这些时刻像受惊的猫一样附着在我的大脑上，拒绝离开。我后悔它们的能力有时感觉像是我个性中最深、最持久的部分。

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:29.890
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Case in point: about 15 years ago, I was riding a train to Toronto with my girlfriend at the time, when she asked me if I could catch the porter's eye and ask him for a glass of 7UP. I didn't like the idea of bothering the train workers. As a group, they put forth a fair bit of gruff, and I did not like receiving the stuff. In fact, even a tiny little bit of gruff would be enough to ruin my weekend.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>举个例子：大约15年前，我和当时的女朋友坐火车去多伦多，她问我能不能引起列车员的注意，帮她要一杯七喜。我不喜欢麻烦铁路工作人员。作为一个群体，他们有点粗鲁，我不喜欢受这种气。事实上，即使是一点点粗鲁也足以毁掉我的周末。

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:40.830
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Still, she was my girlfriend, and they had just handed out salted peanuts, and a drink seemed like a pretty good idea. I would probably even end up taking a sip off of hers.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>不过，她是我女朋友，而且他们刚分发了咸花生，喝杯饮料听起来是个不错的主意。我可能甚至会最后喝一口她的。

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:51.410
<v Jonathan Goldstein>"Why don't you ask him," I said, offering a compromise. And by compromise, I meant I'd sit beside her pretending to be engrossed in a copy of Rolling Stone while she got yelled at.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>“你怎么不问他？”我说，提出一个折中方案。所谓的折中，意思是我会坐在她身边假装沉迷于《滚石》杂志，而她则会被吼。

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:54.990
<v Jonathan Goldstein>"You should," she said. "You're the man."
<v Jonathan Goldstein>“你应该问的，”她说。“你是男人。”

00:06:54.990 --> 00:07:19.410
<v Jonathan Goldstein>She was right about that. I was the man. An 18-year-old man who was spending his first weekend out of town with a woman. When the porter, who must have easily been at least 75, came moseying by, I craned my head into the aisle and said, just like this, "E-- excuse me, but d-- do you think that maybe I could get a 7UP, please, when you get a chance, if that-- if that's possible?"
<v Jonathan Goldstein>她是对的。我是男人。一个18岁的男人，第一次和一个女人在城外度过周末。当那个至少有75岁的列车员晃晃悠悠走过来时，我把头伸进过道，就这样说：“呃——打扰一下，但……你觉得我能不能喝杯七喜，拜托，当你方便的时候，如果那——那可能的话？”

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:24.980
<v Jonathan Goldstein>"I already gave you a drink," he said. "There is no second 7UP."
<v Jonathan Goldstein>“我已经给你喝过饮料了，”他说。“没有第二杯七喜。”

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:39.280
<v Jonathan Goldstein>"No, no, you did," I said. "But that was before the peanuts. Now I need another. Peanuts will do that to a person. Ma-- make them thirsty. I'm-- I'm sorry."
<v Jonathan Goldstein>“不，不，你给了，”我说。“但那是在花生之前。现在我需要再来一杯。花生会让人那样。会让——让人口渴。我——我抱歉。”

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:41.830
<v Jonathan Goldstein>People turned around and looked at me.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>人们转过身看着我。

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:50.620
<v Jonathan Goldstein>"There should be a drink before and after the peanuts," I said. "That would be so great. Don't you think that-- that might be a good idea, maybe?"
<v Jonathan Goldstein>“花生前后都应该有饮料，”我说。“那会太棒了。你不觉得那——那可能是一个好主意吗，也许？”

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:58.600
<v Jonathan Goldstein>All the while as I spoke, my girlfriend sat beside me, falling slowly and imperceptibly more and more out of love with me.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>在我说话的时候，我女朋友坐在我身边，缓慢且不易察觉地越来越不爱我了。

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:08.740
<v Jonathan Goldstein>When he placed the plastic cup of 7UP down on my tray, he told me hoped it would help me, "wash my nuts down."
<v Jonathan Goldstein>当他把那杯塑料杯装的七喜放在我的托盘上时，他告诉我希望这能帮我“把花生冲下去”。

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:45.030
<v Jonathan Goldstein>That night in bed, I realized what I should have replied. "My nuts are washed, old timer. Don't worry about me." Several months later, daydreaming in class, I thought of this. I could have lifted up the cup in a toast and said, "To clean nuts." But at the time, all I could think to do was to look down at the tiny, effervescent world within my cup and say thanks. Within the month, my girlfriend and I would be history.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>那天晚上在床上，我意识到我本该怎么回答。“我的花生已经冲下去了，老兄。别担心我。”几个月后，在课堂上做白日梦时，我想到了这一点。我本可以举杯祝酒说：“为干净的花生干杯。”但当时，我能想到的唯一一件事就是看着杯子里那微小而起泡的世界说谢谢。一个月内，我和我女朋友就成为了过去式。

00:08:45.030 --> 00:09:14.780
<v Jonathan Goldstein>If I could stop time, I would not rob the Bank of England, nor would I spend probably more than an afternoon or two studying the hummingbird in mid-flight. I would use my power to freeze time just before I was about to open my mouth to speak. I would get up from wherever I was and pace. I would pace back and forth and perhaps drink a glass of cold water. And once I had crafted the perfect response and steadied my nerves, I would unfreeze time and continue.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>如果我能停止时间，我不会去抢劫英格兰银行，也不会花超过一个下午的时间研究飞行中的蜂鸟。我会利用我的力量在开口说话前冻结时间。我会从任何地方站起来踱步。我会来回踱步，也许喝一杯冷水。一旦我想出了完美的回答并稳定了情绪，我就会解冻时间并继续。

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:53.590
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Of course, one cannot stop time, but one can stop tape. As an exercise in getting things right, I decided to go through one entire day tape-recording all of my interactions. That way, when the day was over, I would be able to go back over all of my conversations. And by stopping the tape and inserting the right words, I would be able to make everything, finally and for once, perfect. Lord knows saying the right words to people's faces would be far too frightening, but right here, behind their backs on the radio, that would be just right.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>当然，人不能停止时间，但可以停止录音带。作为让事情变得正确的练习，我决定花一整天时间录下我所有的互动。这样，当一天结束时，我就能回顾所有的谈话。通过停止录音带并插入正确的话语，我将能够让一切最终变得完美，哪怕只有一次。天知道当面说对的话太吓人了，但在背后，在广播里，那正合适。

00:09:53.590 --> 00:10:00.800
<v Jonathan Goldstein>So in the morning, I had to go by to my friend Mary's house to babysit her kids. Helen is four, and Katie is two.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>所以早上，我得去我朋友玛丽家看她的孩子。海伦四岁，凯蒂两岁。

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.230
<v Helen>Silly Johnny.
<v Helen>傻强尼。

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:04.140
<v Katie>Silly Johnny.
<v Katie>傻强尼。

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:15.670
<v Jonathan Goldstein>This is what the kids do every time they see me. They start chanting. They chant, "Silly Johnny." For them, that is my name. It is what they call me. Silly Johnny.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>这就是孩子们每次见到我时做的事。她们开始念口诀。她们念：“傻强尼。”对她们来说，那就是我的名字。她们就是那样叫我的。傻强尼。

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:18.110
<v Helen And Katie>Silly Johnny, silly Johnny, silly Johnny, silly Johnny.
<v Helen And Katie>傻强尼，傻强尼，傻强尼，傻强尼。

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:58.060
<v Jonathan Goldstein>OK, hold it right here. There they stand, frozen like porcelain figurines, their mouths frozen wide open, their heads thrown back in cruel delight. And this is what I should have said. I am not the silly one, baby Katie. I could sit down to watch Apocalypse Now with a plate of spicy chicken wings and beer without getting all scarred for life. And I do not have to wear diapers, unless, of course, I choose to. And Helen, can you walk out the door anytime you want, go to an ATM machine, and put a down payment on a leather couch? I don't think so. So who's really silly?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>好了，停在这儿。她们站在那儿，像陶瓷塑像一样僵住，嘴巴张得大大的，头因为残酷的愉悦而向后仰。我本该这样说。我不是傻瓜，小凯蒂。我可以一边看《现代启示录》，一边吃着一盘辣鸡翅喝着啤酒，而不会留下心理阴影。而且我不必穿尿布，当然，除非我选择穿。还有海伦，你能随时走出大门，去自动取款机，给皮沙发付定金吗？我不这么认为。所以谁才是傻瓜？

00:10:58.060 --> 00:10:59.870
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Instead, I said this.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>相反，我说了这个。

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:05.500
<v Helen And Katie>Silly Johnny, silly Johnny, silly Johnny, silly Johnny, silly Johnny.
<v Helen And Katie>傻强尼，傻强尼，傻强尼，傻强尼，傻强尼。

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:08.080
<v Jonathan Goldstein>As you could hear, I said nothing.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>正如你听到的，我什么也没说。

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:19.810
<v Helen>Silly Johnny, silly Johnny, silly John, silly Johnny, silly Johnny, silly--
<v Helen>傻强尼，傻强尼，傻强尼，傻强尼，傻强尼，傻——

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:50.450
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Mary runs a profitable fancy soap shop, and she's been looking for a part-time employee for months. And every time she starts in about how hard it is to find good help these days, I always tell her how I'd be available to work in the store a few afternoons a week. And then Mary will suddenly remember something she left in the oven. Or she'll change the subject by complimenting me on the sporty-looking jersey I'm wearing, which, truthfully, is not sporty at all or for that matter, even a jersey, unless an undershirt can be called a jersey.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>玛丽经营着一家赚钱的高档肥皂店，几个月来她一直在找兼职员工。每次她开始抱怨现在找好帮手有多难时，我总是告诉她我下午有空可以在店里工作几个小时。然后玛丽就会突然想起炉子里放了什么东西。或者她会通过夸奖我穿的运动衫来转移话题，老实说，那衣服一点也不运动，如果内衣也能被称为运动衫的话。

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:58.110
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Having most of the morning to watch the kids and brood, I decided that when Mary got back, I was going to broach the subject straight on.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>大部分早上时间都在看孩子和沉思，我决定玛丽回来后，我要直接切入主题。

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:04.220
<v Jonathan Goldstein>How come you won't have me work for you? You know I would be a good employee, right?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你怎么就不让我为你工作呢？你知道我会是个好员工，对吧？

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:06.340
<v Mary>No, you wouldn't be a good employee.
<v Mary>不，你不会是个好员工。

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:12.490
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Now, you see, most people would just drop the subject right here. But I am not most people.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你看，大多数人到这儿就会放弃这个话题了。但我不是大多数人。

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:13.860
<v Jonathan Goldstein>You really feel that way?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你真的这么觉得吗？

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:38.530
<v Mary>Yes. The first thing is, you pay no attention. You're completely spaced-out. Plus, you look a little bit ratty, and I think that the women who are coming in to ask you advice about fragrance and the different soaps and how well they wash, I don't think you look the type that would be able to give them some good advice. And you don't follow direction. And you can't multitask.
<v Mary>是啊。首先，你不专心。你完全心不在焉。而且，你看起来有点邋遢，我觉得那些进来向你咨询香水、不同肥皂以及它们洗得有多干净的女性，我不觉得你看起来是那种能给她们好建议的人。而且你不听指挥。你也不能一心多用。

00:12:38.530 --> 00:13:14.880
<v Jonathan Goldstein>OK, stop the tape right here.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>好了，把录音带停在这儿。

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:17.380
<v Jonathan Goldstein>You don't respect me, do you?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你不尊重我，是吗？

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:28.100
<v Mary>Um, I think you're a nice person.
<v Mary>嗯，我觉得你是个好人。

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:49.410
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Later in the afternoon, I had to drop by the bookstore and pick up, for reasons I won't go into right here, a book on patent law. So when I get to the bookstore, this guy I know, Billy, is there. He doesn't work at the store, but he's there pretty much every time I go in, leaning against the counter and recommending The Master and Margarita to anyone who will listen.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>下午晚些时候，我得去书店买一本关于专利法的书，原因我不在这里详述。所以当我到书店时，我认识的一个人，比利，在那儿。他不在这家店工作，但每次我去他基本都在，靠在柜台上向任何愿意听的人推荐《大师和玛格丽特》。

00:13:49.410 --> 00:14:06.760
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Anyway, in the back of the store, Billy and I get into this discussion about Lord of The Rings. I'm not even sure how we got onto the subject. Billy is one of those guys who in high school used to hang up maps of Middle-earth in his locker. The kind of guy who could sing songs by Simon & Garfunkel in Elvish.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>总之，在书店后面，我和比利讨论起《指环王》。我甚至不确定我们是怎么聊到这个话题的。比利是那种高中时会在储物柜里挂中土世界地图的家伙。那种能用精灵语唱西蒙与加芬克尔歌曲的家伙。

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:33.620
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I start telling him about how I had just seen the movie, and how Frodo struck me as a little overly emotional about carrying the ring. Sure, the ring tempted people with acts of evil, but so does combining Jack Daniels, insomnia, and the Google search engine. I said I didn't think it would be such a huge deal to carry the ring, at least for a little while. Now, admittedly, our whole argument was ridiculous, but as ridiculous as it was, I was still losing.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我开始告诉他我刚看过这部电影，以及弗罗多在带戒指这件事上给我的印象有点过于情绪化。确实，戒指诱惑人们做出邪恶的行为，但把杰克丹尼、失眠和谷歌搜索引擎结合起来也一样。我说我不觉得带戒指是什么大不了的事，至少带一会儿。现在，不得不承认，我们整个争论很可笑，但尽管可笑，我还是输了。

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:38.010
<v Billy>I give you the Ring of Power, OK? What are you going to do with it, John? What are you doing to do with it?
<v Billy>我把至尊魔戒给你，好吗？你会拿它做什么，约翰？你会拿它做什么？

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:38.830
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I--
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我——

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:55.390
<v Billy>No question about it. You wouldn't pull it off. You couldn't pull it off. You'd die. Your soul would be taken. Finished, end of story. You wouldn't cut it. You wouldn't cut it on the throne. You wouldn't even be a steward of Gondor. You wouldn't even be one of the Rohirrim? And you want to hold the ring? You couldn't hold the ring, John.
<v Billy>毫无疑问。你撑不住的。你做不到。你会死。你的灵魂会被夺走。完了，故事结束。你不行。你在王位上也不行。你甚至做不了刚铎的宰相。你甚至做不了洛汗骑兵？而你还想持有戒指？你持有不了戒指，约翰。

00:14:55.390 --> 00:15:26.300
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Now, if I could have just paused things right here, right at this very moment, paced around a bit, shadow-boxed myself towards the right state of mind, I might have found the gumption to say that I didn't even want the stinking ring because all it does is turn you into a pretentious diva anyway. Everyone who holds it becomes Zsa Zsa Gabor. I would have said that even thinking about this ring stuff was for 14-year-old black-hooded Goth kids who strap suede pouches of 20-sided dice from their belts as fashion accessories.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>现在，如果我能在这里暂停，就在这一刻，踱步一下，对着自己进行影子拳击，调整到正确的心态，我可能就有勇气说我根本不想要这该死的戒指，因为它只会让你变成一个自命不凡的歌剧名伶。每个持有它的人都会变成莎莎·嘉宝。我会说，甚至连考虑这戒指的事儿都是给那些把20面骰子皮袋子挂在皮带上作为时尚配饰的14岁黑斗篷哥特孩子准备的。

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:29.680
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Instead, I answered this way.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>相反，我是这样回答的。

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:35.330
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Um, I don't know. That's a good question. I don't know.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>嗯，我不知道。这是个好问题。我不知道。

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:36.660
<v Ira Glass>To which Billy replied:
<v Ira Glass>对此比利回答道：

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:48.570
<v Billy>You don't know? You don't-- there's even a question? No, Jonathan, you couldn't hold the One Ring of Power. I wouldn't trust you for a second with the Ring of Power. Not for a second.
<v Billy>你不知道？你不知道——这还需要问吗？不，乔纳森，你持有不了至尊魔戒。我一秒钟都不会信任你持有至尊魔戒。一秒钟都不会。

00:15:48.570 --> 00:16:04.110
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Then I started to backpedal, saying that he was probably right, that I was ill-equipped to hold the ring. I was weak-willed and had little sense of community. I then said that he, Billy, was probably better suited to the task of ring bearer. To which Billy responded:
<v Jonathan Goldstein>然后我开始退缩，说他可能是对的，我没能力持有戒指。我意志薄弱，几乎没有集体意识。我接着说他，比利，可能更适合当持戒人。对此比利回答道：

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:19.540
<v Billy>I wouldn't touch the ring, John. OK? Gandalf wouldn't touch the ring. Elrond wouldn't touch the ring. Fools like Boromir tried to touch the ring, but they died. I cannot handle the ring. I am not of Hobbit stock. I don't have that kind of heartiness.
<v Billy>我才不会碰那个戒指，约翰。好吗？甘道夫不会碰戒指。埃尔隆德不会碰戒指。像博罗米尔那样的傻瓜试图碰戒指，但他们死了。我处理不了戒指。我不是霍比特人。我没有那种坚韧的心。

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:22.610
<v Jonathan Goldstein>OK, stop right here.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>好了，停在这儿。

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:41.850
<v Jonathan Goldstein>No, Billy, you don't have that kind of heartiness. You wear yellow sweatpants, and your hands are as smooth as peeled tangerines. You possess the unearthly habit of pulling jaw breakers out of your mouth every five seconds to check what color they are. And whenever you burp, you're compelled to simultaneously speak the word, "Ralph."
<v Jonathan Goldstein>不，比利，你没有那种坚韧的心。你穿着黄色运动裤，你的手像剥了皮的橘子一样光滑。你有一种每隔五秒钟就把下巴糖从嘴里拿出来检查它们颜色的怪癖。每当你打嗝，你都忍不住同时说出“拉尔夫”这个词。

00:16:41.850 --> 00:17:17.060
<v Jonathan Goldstein>But if you're standing there with Billy, looking into his eyes, frozen or not, of course, you don't say anything so mean, despite the fact that you want to. We all carry the ring around inside of us, and that ring is our capacity for wrongdoing. And both he and I, in some way, wish that this capacity could be removed from within us and pounded out into a shiny ring that could be passed around from person to person, allowing us to ease the burden of our urges. Such urges, say, as wanting to voice the really mean things we really think, but in the end, that would be appalling.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>但如果你和比利站在一起，看着他的眼睛，无论是否冻结，当然，你不会说这么刻薄的话，尽管你想这么说。我们每个人内心都带着戒指，那戒指就是我们做坏事的能力。我们两个都在某种程度上希望这种能力能从内心移除，并被捶打成一枚闪亮的戒指，可以在人与人之间传递，让我们减轻冲动的负担。比如想把我们真正想到的刻薄话大声说出来的冲动，但归根结底，那太可怕了。

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:21.300
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And rather than voicing any of this, I said, instead:
<v Jonathan Goldstein>而不是把这些说出来，我反而说了：

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:27.660
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Um, I thought-- I don't know.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>嗯，我想——我不知道。

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:47.490
<v Ira Glass>Silly Johnny. Silly Johnny.
<v Ira Glass>傻强尼。傻强尼。

00:17:47.490 --> 00:18:12.710
<v Ira Glass>In the evening, I had dinner with my parents. I was going to be leaving to New York in a couple of days. And all evening, my father kept bringing up this bottle of vodka he wanted me to buy for him. And all evening, my mother kept cutting him off and yelling at him, louder and angrier as the night wore on. She told him it was impossible for me to get him the bottle, simply impossible. Why she thought it was impossible, I have no idea.
<v Ira Glass>晚上，我和父母一起吃了晚饭。我两天后就要去纽约了。整晚，我父亲一直提起那瓶他想让我给他买的伏特加。整晚，我母亲一直打断他并对他大吼，随着夜晚的推移，声音越来越大，越来越愤怒。她告诉他我给他买那瓶酒是不可能的，简直是不可能。她为什么觉得不可能，我一点也不知道。

00:18:12.710 --> 00:18:19.770
<v Ira Glass>But anyway, eventually, my father dropped the subject. But then, after dinner was finished, he started once more.
<v Ira Glass>但总之，最终，我父亲放弃了这个话题。但在晚饭吃完后，他又开始了一次。

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:25.500
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Father>It was a good meal, yeah. It was a good meal. But also I would like you to bring me back another bottle of that--
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Father>这顿饭很不错，是啊。这是顿好饭。但我也希望你给我带回另一瓶那种——

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:29.270
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Mother>Could you understand that he can't? It's impossible. Stop that--
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Mother>你能理解他办不到吗？这不可能。别再——

00:18:29.270 --> 00:18:29.800
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Father>It's imposs--
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Father>这不可能——

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:32.090
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Mother>Yes. Go on out, and buy yourself what you want.
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Mother>是的。出去，买你自己想要的东西吧。

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:34.360
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Father>You can't Smirnoff's 100 proof--
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Father>你不能把斯米诺100度——

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:38.100
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Mother>He can't get it. He's not going to schlep bottles from New York City for you.
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Mother>他买不到。他不会为你从纽约市拖运瓶子的。

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:42.260
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Father>All right. I have to say one thing, that if I have certain pleasures in life--
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Father>好吧。我得说一件事，如果我在生活中确实有一些乐趣——

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:47.610
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Mother>Who says you need pleasures? You don't need any pleasures. You're too old already for pleasures.
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Mother>谁说你需要乐趣？你不需要任何乐趣。你年纪太大了，不需要什么乐趣了。

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:51.940
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Father>She diminishes my manhood. And she knows where to hit.
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Father>她贬低我作为一个男人的尊严。而且她知道打哪里最痛。

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:57.090
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Mother>OK, Johnny, just have some pineapple now, sweetheart. Sit down and relax. Come eat something.
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Mother>好了，强尼，现在吃点菠萝，亲爱的。坐下来放松一下。来吃点东西吧。

00:18:57.090 --> 00:19:13.760
<v Jonathan Goldstein>OK, let's just stop right here. What do you say to that? Even after days of rumination, I still got nothing.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>好了，我们就停在这儿。你对此能说什么？即使经过几天的反复思量，我还是什么也说不出。

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:28.370
<v Jonathan Goldstein>After dinner, my mother drove me to the bus stop. And in the car along the way, she spoke of how her job was going. My mother works at a daycare, and she really, really loves the kids. She was telling me about how her favorite thing is when the children let her hug them.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>晚饭后，我母亲开车送我去汽车站。在车上，她谈起了她的工作进展。我母亲在一家托儿所工作，她真的、真的很爱那些孩子。她告诉我她最喜欢的事情是孩子们让她拥抱他们。

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:34.570
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Mother>You used to be so mean. I used to beg you for hugs, and you wouldn't give me any. You used to not give hugs.
<v Jonathan Goldstein's Mother>你以前总是那么刻薄。我曾经乞求你的拥抱，但你从不给我。你以前从来不给拥抱。

00:19:34.570 --> 00:20:02.970
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I realize now that it must have been difficult to have a child who never let you touch them. In old photographs, you could see me inching away uncomfortably from her hands on my shoulders. At my grade six prom, at the beginning of the mother-son dance, which I still remember as Barbara Streisand and Kris Kristofferson's "You Don't Bring Me Flowers," I refused to dance with her. The whole notion of dancing with your mother in, of all places, your school's gymnasium struck me as unwholesome.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我现在意识到，有一个从不让你触碰的孩子一定很难。在旧照片里，你可以看到我因为她在我的肩膀上而感到不适，正一点点退缩。在我六年级的舞会上，在母子舞的开始，我至今仍记得那是芭芭拉·史翠珊和克里斯·克里斯托佛森的《你不带我去看花》，我拒绝和她跳舞。在那种地方——你学校的健身房里——和母亲跳舞的概念让我觉得不健全。

00:20:02.970 --> 00:20:27.790
<v Jonathan Goldstein>If there had been a pause button on the dashboard, and I could stop time, stop the flow of traffic entirely so that all the cars beside us on the road became these blurry Monet lily pads, and I could open the passenger door and step out to pace around among them, if I could have sat down on the hood of the car and stared at my mother's face, then maybe I might then have been able to say something better than what I did say, which was this:
<v Jonathan Goldstein>如果仪表盘上有一个暂停键，我能停止时间，完全停止交通流，让路上的车都变成莫奈画中模糊的睡莲，如果我能打开副驾驶门走出去在它们中间踱步，如果我能坐在车盖上盯着母亲的脸，那么也许我那时就能说出比我当时所说的更好的话，我当时说的是：

00:20:27.790 --> 00:20:46.410
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It's really nice the way you talk about the kids in your school. It's-- it-- it-- you know, the-the parents are lucky to have you looking after their kids. You really-- you know, you give them love, you know, which is something that you can't-- you can't really pay someone to love your-- your kids the way that, you know, the way that you would want them to be loved.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你谈论学校里的孩子们的方式真的很棒。那是——那——你知道，家长们有你照看他们的孩子真是幸运。你真的——你知道，你给了他们爱，你知道，这是你不能——你不能真正付钱让某人去爱你的——你的孩子，以那种，你知道，那种你想让他们被爱的方式。

00:20:46.410 --> 00:21:00.460
<v Ira Glass>Even as I was saying it, I knew there was a better, more difficult thing to say, namely, I am sorry for never hugging you. Although what I did say wasn't exactly right, at that moment, it felt like the least wrong.
<v Ira Glass>即使在我说的时候，我也知道有一句更好、更难说的话，那就是：对不起，我从未拥抱过你。虽然我所说的不完全正确，但在那一刻，我觉得那是最小的错误。

00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:23.160
<v Ira Glass>Life isn't about saying the right thing anyway. And it is certainly not about tape-recording everything so that you have to endure it more than once. Life is about failing. It is about letting the tape play. It is about eating the pineapple you are being force-fed, and, to the best of your ability, enjoying it.
<v Ira Glass>生活本来就不是关于说正确的话。当然，生活也不是关于把一切都录下来，这样你就不得不忍受不止一次。生活是关于失败。是关于让录音带继续播放。是关于吃掉你被强迫喂的菠萝，并尽你所能享受它。

00:21:23.160 --> 00:21:30.760
<v Ira Glass>Jonathan Goldstein, author of the novel Lenny Bruce is Dead and host of the CBC radio program Wiretap.
<v Ira Glass>乔纳森·戈尔茨坦，小说《莱尼·布鲁斯已死》的作者，加拿大广播公司广播节目《窃听》的主持人。

00:21:30.760 --> 00:22:13.320
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "FLY LIKE AN EAGLE" BY THE STEVE MILLER BAND]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 史蒂夫·米勒乐队的《像鹰一样飞翔》]

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:41.250
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, In The Bush Leagues. When he was in his early 20's, barely out of school, at the very first political event of his life, Charles Monroe-Kane got into a shouting match with the leader of the free world. And he pretty much lost that one. This was in the early 1990s. He just left the religious school where he had been studying to be a minister. And he moved to Amsterdam, where he fell in with a bunch of anarchists. He edited an environmental newspaper called the Green Tree News.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，小联盟。20出头的时候，刚走出校门，在他人生中第一次政治活动上，查尔斯·门罗-凯恩与自由世界的领导人发生了一场争吵。他基本上输了那场争吵。那是90年代初。他刚离开学习成为牧师的宗教学校。他搬到了阿姆斯特丹，在那里他结识了一群无政府主义者。他编辑了一份名为《绿树新闻》的环境报纸。

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:48.620
<v Charles Monroe-kane>And I wanted to be an activist, so that's the whole reason I think I wanted to be a minister. I was into liberation theology. That's what I wanted to be.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我想成为一名活动家，所以我认为这就是我想要成为牧师的全部原因。我热衷于解放神学。那才是我想要成为的人。

00:22:48.620 --> 00:23:07.090
<v Charles Monroe-kane>And I was there about four or five weeks. And the G7 meeting was happening in Munich, Germany. President Bush-- former President Bush, or President Senior, or Dad, whatever you want to call him-- he was president. So we went down like a good activist would do. It was in Munich, Germany. We went down to protest. And I had only been doing this for four or five weeks, so I was kind of down.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我在那儿待了四五周。七国集团会议正在德国慕尼黑举行。布什总统——前总统布什，或者布什老爹，或者爸爸，随你怎么叫他——他是总统。所以我们就去了，像个好的活动家应该做的那样。那是德国慕尼黑。我们去抗议。我做这个才四五周，所以我当时有点……

00:23:07.090 --> 00:23:20.320
<v Charles Monroe-kane>It was my first political action ever. I was sleeping in the back of a bus with a bunch of dread-locked people. I had my hair down over my shoulders, and I had just gotten my ear pierced. I had a peace sign in my earring. You can imagine the person, right?
<v Charles Monroe-kane>这是我人生第一次政治行动。我和一群留着脏辫的人睡在一辆大巴车后面。我的头发垂在肩膀上，我刚打了耳洞。我的耳环上有一个和平标志。你能想象那个人，对吧？

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:44.590
<v Charles Monroe-kane>And so basically, we were going down to protest, and I had this press pass. I'm a bit of a talker, bit of a schmoozer. Met some people there, and found out that there are seven levels of press passes. I had level number one, which got you nothing but some faxes. Level number two got you nothing but some faxes, but also got you this buffet once a day. And now I'm sleeping in a bus. I'm like, well, hell, I'm not stupid. So I fandango my way in-- press pass number two.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>基本上，我们要去抗议，我有一张媒体证。我挺能说的，挺擅长交际。在那儿遇到了一些人，发现媒体证有七个等级。我有一级证，除了能收到一些传真外什么都没有。二级证除了能收到一些传真外什么都没有，但每天能享用一次自助餐。而现在我睡在大巴里。我想，去你的，我不傻。所以我一路忽悠——弄到了二级媒体证。

00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:59.750
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Well, of course, food brought me to number three and number four. Now, number five got you a hotel room. I thought, well, my friends could use this. Because we were starting to smell on the second day. So I kept BS-ing my way all the way through just to get these press passes.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>嗯，当然，食物把我带到了三级和四级。现在，五级证能让你住进酒店房间。我想，嗯，我的朋友们能用到这个。因为我们在第二天就开始有味道了。所以我一直胡吹乱侃，一路混到了这些媒体证。

00:23:59.750 --> 00:24:01.320
<v Ira Glass>Wait, so you get the room?
<v Ira Glass>等等，所以你拿到了房间？

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:12.240
<v Charles Monroe-kane>All the way to the sixth level, yeah. I get the room. We get in there-- and the food was awesome, by the way, on the sixth level-- and the suite was-- it was a decent-- it was almost like a suite. It was a decent size where all of us could stay for free.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>一直到六级，是的。我拿到了房间。我们进去那里——顺便说一句，六级的食物很棒——套房是——这算是个不错的——它几乎就像一个套房。它大小适中，我们所有人都可以免费住在那儿。

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:32.420
<v Charles Monroe-kane>And then what happens is I'm with some people who later became very good friends of mine, but at the time, I didn't know them very well. A friend named Paxus and some others who were high level at Friends of The Earth and Greenpeace. These people are like, "Do you realize what you have? You have a level six press pass at the G7 meeting. You have access to all these places and all these things."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>然后发生的事是，我和一些后来成为我很好朋友的人在一起，但当时，我不太了解他们。一个叫帕克苏斯的朋友和其他一些人在“地球之友”和“绿色和平组织”担任高层。这些人说：“你知道你有什么吗？你在七国集团会议上有六级媒体证。你可以进入所有这些地方和所有这些事情。”

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:33.070
<v Charles Monroe-kane>"OK, that's cool."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>“好吧，那很酷。”

00:24:33.070 --> 00:24:43.550
<v Charles Monroe-kane>He said, "But what you don't have is you don't have a White House press pass. That's the top level." So he's like, "You know what you ought to do? You ought to try the next level. You should try to get a White House press pass."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>他说，“但你没有的是你没有白宫媒体证。那是最高等级。”所以他说，“你知道你应该做什么吗？你应该尝试下一级。你应该试着弄一张白宫媒体证。”

00:24:43.550 --> 00:24:44.130
<v Charles Monroe-kane>I said, "Why?"
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我说，“为什么？”

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:55.260
<v Charles Monroe-kane>He said, "Because you could be live at a press conference. You can protest. Tomorrow, George Bush, at 11 o'clock in the morning"-- this is in the afternoon the day before-- "he's giving a live press conference on CNN."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>他说，“因为你可以在新闻发布会上直播。你可以抗议。明天，乔治·布什，在上午11点”——这是前一天下午——“他要在CNN上举行现场新闻发布会。”

00:24:55.260 --> 00:24:58.520
<v Charles Monroe-kane>I was like, "Oh, I never thought of it. I've never done an action before."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我说，“哦，我从来没想过。我以前从来没做过这种行动。”

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:07.480
<v Charles Monroe-kane>So I went, and I went to this beer garden where I knew some of the people who made the decisions were. And I met a man named Marlin Fitzwater-- I think a lot of people might know who he is-- and he's sitting there drinking. But I didn't know who the hell he was.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>所以我去了，我去了那个啤酒花园，我知道一些做决定的人在那儿。我遇到了一个叫马林·菲茨沃特的人——我想很多人可能知道他是谁——他坐在那儿喝酒。但我不知道这家伙是谁。

00:25:07.480 --> 00:25:09.010
<v Ira Glass>The White House Press Secretary.
<v Ira Glass>白宫新闻秘书。

00:25:09.010 --> 00:25:09.460
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Yeah, yeah.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>是啊，是啊。

00:25:09.460 --> 00:25:10.900
<v Ira Glass>The actual White House Press Secretary.
<v Ira Glass>真正的白宫新闻秘书。

00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:16.450
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Because that's the step. Because that's a higher level. The president's got a different security issue.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>因为那是步骤。因为那是更高的级别。总统有不同的安全问题。

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:29.100
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Well, here's the logic for him, right? He's already assumed I've been checked. Remember, he already knows I have the sixth level press pass, so I'm not like I'm an anarchist in Amsterdam or something like that, for Christ's sake. So I think that he wasn't too worried about that.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>好吧，他的逻辑是这样的，对吧？他已经假设我被检查过了。记住，他已经知道我有六级媒体证，所以我不是那种来自阿姆斯特丹的无政府主义者，看在上帝的份上。所以我认为他对此并不太担心。

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:54.340
<v Charles Monroe-kane>So then, anyway, he gives me a press pass. It's very exciting. I go back to my hotel room with a bunch of naked, dirty hippies, and I show these people the press pass. And they flip out. They're like, "You're kidding. You got it." I don't understand the ramifications of what's about to happen at all. So basically, they're like, "Well, tomorrow morning, President Bush, at 11 o'clock in the morning, is giving this conference, a press conference live on CNN. You should-- you have a press pass to go there-- I think that you should do something."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>总之，他给了我一张媒体证。这太令人兴奋了。我带着一群赤身裸体、肮脏的嬉皮士回到我的酒店房间，给这些人看媒体证。他们都疯了。他们说：“你在开玩笑吧。你拿到了。”我一点也不明白接下来要发生什么的影响。所以基本上，他们说，“嗯，明天早上，布什总统，在上午11点，要举行这个新闻发布会，CNN直播。你应该——你有媒体证去那里——我觉得你应该做点什么。”

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:55.690
<v Charles Monroe-kane>I was like, "Well, I agree."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我说，“嗯，我同意。”

00:25:55.690 --> 00:26:06.170
<v Charles Monroe-kane>So then I sit down with these guys, these guys now who are like-- all the hippie, dirty activists are out of the room, and now I've got the Greenpeace, Friends of the Earth people who know what they're talking about guys. And they're like, "Have you thought about what you're going to say?"
<v Charles Monroe-kane>于是我和这些家伙坐下来，这些家伙现在——所有肮脏的嬉皮活动家都出去了，现在我有“地球之友”和“绿色和平组织”那些懂行的人。他们说：“你想好要说什么了吗？”

00:26:06.170 --> 00:26:07.790
<v Charles Monroe-kane>And I was like, "No, not really."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我说，“不，还没真正想好。”

00:26:07.790 --> 00:26:21.880
<v Charles Monroe-kane>So we talked about it. And they said, "The most important thing to do is two things. Keep your hands away from your body while you do this, so you don't get shot. Because they might think it's an attempted assassination or something. And you're going to get 20 to 30 seconds max. That's all you're going to get, so know what you're going to say."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>所以我们谈论了它。他们说，“最重要的事情是做两件事。当你做这件事时，把你的手放在身体外面，这样你才不会被枪击。因为他们可能会认为这是一次暗杀企图之类的。你最多只有20到30秒。这就是你所有的全部时间，所以知道你要说什么。”

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:27.510
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait. These are the two pieces of advice they give you? Piece of advice number one is, don't get yourself shot? Like, that's--
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。这就是他们给你的两条建议？建议第一条是，不要让自己被枪击？就像，那是——

00:26:27.510 --> 00:26:48.040
<v Charles Monroe-kane>It's good advice, though. And I took it. Because they were like, "Keep your hands away from your body." They said, "Don't wear a watch. Roll up your sleeves, or have on a short-sleeve shirt. You have to be careful. You're in a small room. You're standing up-- basically, you're going to stand up on a chair. Situate yourself in the middle of the room, so you're hard to get at by the Secret Service guys, who are going to, in 15 or 30 seconds, drag you away and arrest you. So what are you going to say?"
<v Charles Monroe-kane>这可是个好建议。我也接受了。因为他们说，“把手放在身体外面。”他们说，“不要戴手表。卷起袖子，或者穿短袖衬衫。你必须小心。你在一个小房间里。你站着——基本上，你要站到椅子上。把自己安置在房间中间，这样特勤局的人很难抓住你，他们会在15或30秒内把你拖走并逮捕。所以你要说什么？”

00:26:48.040 --> 00:26:50.530
<v Charles Monroe-kane>And I thought, "Well, all I know is Christianity."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我想，“嗯，我唯一懂的就是基督教。”

00:26:50.530 --> 00:26:53.920
<v Charles Monroe-kane>And they said, "Well, that's OK. You can go with that. Americans relate to that."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>他们说，“嗯，那没关系。你可以用那个。美国人对此有共鸣。”

00:26:53.920 --> 00:27:00.550
<v Charles Monroe-kane>And I said, "You know, I've always wanted to be one of the Old Testament prophets. I thought that would be really cool. That's kind of a life dream. That was what I wanted to be."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我说，“你知道，我一直想成为旧约中的先知之一。我觉得那会很酷。那算是人生的梦想。那才是我想要成为的人。”

00:27:00.550 --> 00:27:10.450
<v Ira Glass>Now, wait, wait. Now when you say this to a bunch of real activists, "I've always wanted to be one of the Old Testament prophets," is that pretty much where they roll their eyes and slowly shake their heads and look at the ground? They just think, "Oh, what have we gotten ourselves into here?"
<v Ira Glass>现在，等等，等等。当你对一群真正的活动家说“我一直想成为旧约中的先知之一”时，那是他们翻白眼、慢慢摇头看着地面的那种时刻吗？他们只会想，“哦，我们到底卷进什么鬼东西里了？”

00:27:10.450 --> 00:27:23.740
<v Charles Monroe-kane>No, I think they were more like, "Jesus, this dude's got a White House press pass in his hand, and he is such an idiot. He's so not prepared that we've got to help this kid out. Because we don't have the press pass. He does, and he's the only one who's going to get in."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>不，我觉得他们更多是想，“上帝啊，这家伙手里拿着一张白宫媒体证，但他是个彻头彻尾的白痴。他准备得太不充分了，我们得帮帮这孩子。因为我们没有媒体证。只有他有，他是唯一一个能进去的人。”

00:27:23.740 --> 00:27:40.980
<v Charles Monroe-kane>And so I went to the Bible. And my favorite profit was Jeremiah. And Jeremiah had a certain style. So I think, "Here's what I want to do. I want to get some ashes. I'm going to put some ashes on my head. I want to rip my shirt off, rip it open [MAKES RIPPING SOUND] like that. And I'm going to give him a line from the Old Testament. A good line."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>于是我求助于圣经。我最喜欢的先知是耶利米。耶利米有一种特定的风格。所以我心想，“这是我想做的。我想弄点灰烬。我要在头上撒点灰。我要把衬衫撕开 [做撕裂声音] 像那样。然后我要给他念一句旧约里的台词。一句好台词。”

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:44.220
<v Charles Monroe-kane>And they said, "Well, maybe you shouldn't quote the Bible. You should do it in that style, something from your thing."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>他们说，“嗯，也许你不应该引用圣经。你应该用那种风格，做一些你自己的东西。”

00:27:44.220 --> 00:28:04.360
<v Charles Monroe-kane>So I thought about it, thought about it, and I came up with this great line. "The homeless in the trees are mourning your economic decisions. Repent, dear King, or go to hell." I thought, "Now, that's a good line." That's good. It's about him, but it takes inanimate objects into effect. I'm an environmental activist as well. So I said, "That's what I'm going to do."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>所以我想了又想，想到了这句棒极了的台词。“树上的无家可归者在哀悼你的经济决策。忏悔吧，亲爱的国王，否则下地狱吧。”我想，“现在，那是一句好台词。”那很好。那是关于他的，但它把无生命物体考虑在内。我也是环境活动家。所以我说，“这就是我要做的。”

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:06.410
<v Ira Glass>Now Charles, can we just pause the story right here--
<v Ira Glass>现在查尔斯，我们可以把故事暂停在这儿吗——

00:28:06.410 --> 00:28:06.760
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Absolutely.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>当然。

00:28:06.760 --> 00:28:16.810
<v Ira Glass>And let's move to the present, a dozen years later. Now, when you think about that quote now, as the adult you are, how do you view that quote now?
<v Ira Glass>让我们跳到现在的十二年后。当你作为一个成年人回顾那句引语时，你现在怎么看那句话？

00:28:16.810 --> 00:28:32.850
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Oh, my god, it's the most embarrassing choice of anything I would ever use in my life. If I had 30 seconds to choose now, I think I would say something maybe a little bit more, a little less, whatever-- symbolic, I don't know. But that's what I had. I was 22, and that's all I knew.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>噢，天哪，这是我人生中最尴尬的选择。如果我现在有30秒的时间选择，我想我会说一些稍微多一点，稍微少一点，不管是什么——象征性的，我不知道。但那就是我当时拥有的。我才22岁，那就是我所知道的一切。

00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:43.180
<v Charles Monroe-kane>So I practiced it over and over again. "The homeless in the trees are mourning your economic decisions. Repent, dear King, or go to hell. The homeless in the trees are mourning your economic decisions. Repent, dear King, or go to hell." Hands away from my body.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>所以我反复练习。 “树上的无家可归者在哀悼你的经济决策。忏悔吧，亲爱的国王，否则下地狱吧。树上的无家可归者在哀悼你的经济决策。忏悔吧，亲爱的国王，否则下地狱吧。” 手放在身体外面。

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:53.280
<v Charles Monroe-kane>But then one of the activists guys came up with a great idea, this American guy. He was like, "Well, don't rip your clothes because that's going to get misunderstood. You should rip an American flag in half. And that should be the cloth that you rip."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>但后来其中一个活动家家伙想出了个好主意，这个美国家伙。他说，“嗯，别撕你的衣服，因为那会被误解。你应该把一面美国国旗撕成两半。那应该是你撕开的布料。”

00:28:53.280 --> 00:29:02.930
<v Charles Monroe-kane>I said, "That's a great idea." So I'm going to put ashes on my forehead, I'm going to rip an American flag in half, I'm going to read that statement, yell it out with all the passion I can muster, get arrested, and awesome!
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我说，“那是个好主意。” 所以我要把灰撒在额头上，我要把一面美国国旗撕成两半，我要念出那段声明，用我能鼓起的全部热情喊出来，被逮捕，简直太棒了！

00:29:02.930 --> 00:29:06.380
<v Ira Glass>Wait. Have you ever-- you've lived in the United States of America, right?
<v Ira Glass>等等。你曾经——你住在美国，对吧？

00:29:06.380 --> 00:29:07.220
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Yeah.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>是的。

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:11.110
<v Ira Glass>Are you familiar with how people feel about the ripping in half of the American flag?
<v Ira Glass>你熟悉人们对把美国国旗撕成两半的感受吗？

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:14.520
<v Charles Monroe-kane>That's probably part of the reason he liked and I liked it, I think, at the time.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>那可能就是他喜欢它、我也喜欢它的原因之一，我想，当时。

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:17.500
<v Ira Glass>But weren't you trying to win the sympathies of your countrymen?
<v Ira Glass>但你不是在试图赢得同胞的同情吗？

00:29:17.500 --> 00:29:29.050
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Yeah, I think I was. But I also think I was-- I don't know what I was trying to do, man-- I was 22. When you were 22, did you know what you were trying to do? Good Lord, I don't know I was trying to do.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>是啊，我想我是在试图。但我还觉得我是在——我不知道我在试图做什么，伙计——我才22岁。你22岁的时候，你知道你在做什么吗？天哪，我不知道我在做什么。

00:29:29.050 --> 00:29:52.660
<v Charles Monroe-kane>So I couldn't sleep that night. They were right. I couldn't sleep. I was a little nervous. And they got me a shirt and a pair of pants. Some shoes that fit me. We had to go to different activists to find it all. A nice tie. I took the earring out. They had a lawyer for me. I gave him my passport. Nothing in my pockets. But shoved down the front of my pants is an American flag-- partly ripped on the top, so I could easily rip it in half-- and some ashes in my pocket.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>所以我那天晚上没睡着。他们是对的。我睡不着。我有点紧张。他们给我弄了一件衬衫和一条裤子。一些合脚的鞋子。我们不得不去找不同的活动家才凑齐。一条漂亮的领带。我摘掉了耳环。他们为我找了个律师。我交出了护照。口袋里什么都没有。但我裤子前面塞着一面美国国旗——顶部已经撕开了一点，这样我就可以很容易地把它撕成两半——口袋里还有些灰烬。

00:29:52.660 --> 00:30:08.530
<v Charles Monroe-kane>And this quote burned into my brain. It was like a zen-- I don't even know what zen is at the time-- I do now. But at the time, it was a zen mantra. "The homeless in the trees are mourning your economic decisions. Repent, dear King, or go to hell." And that's all I had in my mind.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>这句话刻进了我的大脑。那就像一个禅——我当时甚至不知道什么是禅——我现在知道了。但当时，那是一个禅的口诀。“树上的无家可归者在哀悼你的经济决策。忏悔吧，亲爱的国王，否则下地狱吧。” 我脑子里就只有这些。

00:30:08.530 --> 00:30:26.970
<v Charles Monroe-kane>So I go into the press conference saying, you know-- and a former Christian kid, here I am. So I sit down. I situate myself in the middle of the room. There's about 44, 45 people. And I remember counting them. I was nervous, so I counted all the people. My throat was dry, and I thought I was going to pass out. And I didn't want to throw up. I knew that would be bad. That wouldn't be a good symbol. And I didn't want to pass out.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>所以我走进新闻发布会时说，你知道——作为一个前基督教的孩子，我在这里。所以我坐下。我把自己安置在房间中间。大约有44、45个人。我记得我数了他们。我当时很紧张，所以我数了所有人。我的喉咙很干，我想我要晕过去了。我不想要呕吐。我知道那会很糟。那不是一个好象征。我也不想晕过去。

00:30:26.970 --> 00:30:30.700
<v Charles Monroe-kane>So it's coming up to the point. My heart's aflutter.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>所以快到那个点了。我的心在狂跳。

00:30:30.700 --> 00:30:44.920
<v Ira Glass>Well, let me stop you right there. Because we have here in the studio a videotape of this day, July 8, 1992. We got this courtesy of the Vanderbilt University Television Archives. And you have never seen this?
<v Ira Glass>好了，我在这儿打断你。因为我们在演播室里有这一天，1992年7月8日的录像带。这是范德比尔特大学电视档案馆提供的。你从没看过这个？

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:51.250
<v Charles Monroe-kane>No, I've never seen it. I've never even seen the highlights from any of the news. All I've seen is newspaper stuff. I'm really nervous about watching this.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>不，我从没看过。我甚至没看过任何新闻片段。我只见过报纸上的东西。看这个我真的很紧张。

00:30:51.250 --> 00:30:53.680
<v Ira Glass>OK. Let's roll the tape.
<v Ira Glass>好吧。放带子吧。

00:30:53.680 --> 00:30:59.750
<v News Anchor>Right now, let's go live to Munich to hear President Bush's comments at the close of the G7 summit.
<v News Anchor>现在，让我们连线慕尼黑，听听布什总统在七国集团峰会结束时的评论。

00:30:59.750 --> 00:31:02.700
<v Charles Monroe-kane>I totally remember that, him coming in. That was intense.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我完全记得那次，他走进来。那太紧张了。

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:04.230
<v President George H.w. Bush>--the last three days discussing the responsibilities and opportunities that we had--
<v President George H.w. Bush>——过去三天讨论我们所承担的责任和机会——

00:31:04.230 --> 00:31:13.620
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Because that's the point where I'm sitting there with a flag between my legs I'm going rip in half and yell at the man, the President of the United States. Oh my god, it makes me want to pass out. All right. Sorry.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>因为那是我坐着，裤子里塞着一面我要撕成两半并对那个人、美国总统大喊的旗子的时候。天哪，那让我想晕过去。好吧。抱歉。

00:31:13.620 --> 00:31:25.470
<v President George H.w. Bush>--sustaining political reform. I would cite five key accomplishments at the Munich Economic Summit. We've succeeded in achieving a solid consensus on strengthening--
<v President George H.w. Bush>——维持政治改革。我想引用慕尼黑经济峰会的五项关键成就。我们成功地在加强方面达成了坚实的共识——

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:29.660
<v Ira Glass>Now, he's barely spoken for a half minute. He's still in his opening statement.
<v Ira Glass>现在，他才说了半分钟不到。他还在开场白阶段。

00:31:29.660 --> 00:31:31.470
<v George H.w. Bush>--United States, Japan,
<v George H.w. Bush>——美国，日本，

00:31:31.470 --> 00:31:32.040
<v George H.w. Bush>[YELLING IN THE BACKGROUND]
<v George H.w. Bush>[背景中的尖叫声]

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:33.890
