<v Ira Glass>现在镜头移开了。

00:31:34.680 --> 00:31:37.650
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>Repent. We mourn your decisions here. You're not giving us your voice.
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>忏悔吧。我们在哀悼你的决策。你没给我们你的声音。

00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:44.580
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Oh my god, look at that kid. Wow, you can't even hear the great line, "The homeless in the trees are mourning your decisions here."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>天哪，看看那个孩子。哇，你甚至听不清那句伟大的台词，“树上的无家可归者在哀悼你的决策。”

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:47.900
<v Ira Glass>You can't hear it all. You can't hear it at all. All you hear is the president.
<v Ira Glass>你一点也听不见。你一点也听不见。你只听得到总统。

00:31:47.900 --> 00:31:52.760
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Oh, I didn't even notice that. I didn't even know that. What if nobody heard it? I guess not. This is the tape.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>哦，我甚至没注意到这点。我甚至不知道这点。要是没人听到呢？我想没有。这就是带子。

00:31:52.760 --> 00:31:54.670
<v Ira Glass>This is the tape. I don't think anybody heard it.
<v Ira Glass>这就是带子。我觉得没人听到。

00:31:54.670 --> 00:31:56.280
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Wow. That's intense.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>哇。那太紧张了。

00:31:56.280 --> 00:31:56.820
<v President George H.w. Bush>I'm trying to give--
<v President George H.w. Bush>我正试图给出——

00:31:56.820 --> 00:31:58.090
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>[INAUDIBLE] your voice in the US.
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>[听不清] 你在美国的声音。

00:31:58.090 --> 00:32:02.160
<v President George H.w. Bush>I'm trying to give you my voice right now. And if you'd be quiet, maybe you could hear it.
<v President George H.w. Bush>我正试图现在就把我的声音给你。如果你安静点，也许你能听见。

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:03.230
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>But you're not giving it to us [INAUDIBLE].
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>但你没给我们你的声音 [听不清]。

00:32:03.230 --> 00:32:06.420
<v President George H.w. Bush>Well, would you please sit down? We're in the middle of a press conference here.
<v President George H.w. Bush>你是谁，你代表谁？

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:07.410
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>You're not giving us your voice there.
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>你没在那里给我们你的声音。

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:09.300
<v President George H.w. Bush>Well, what's your question, sir?
<v President George H.w. Bush>好吧，你的问题是什么，先生？

00:32:09.300 --> 00:32:10.320
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>I'm under 25, and I want to know--
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>我不到25岁，我想知道——

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:11.820
<v President George H.w. Bush>Well, we can tell that.
<v President George H.w. Bush>嗯，我们看得出来。

00:32:11.820 --> 00:32:13.350
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Nice. Good for him. He got me on that one.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>好。干得好。他在那一点上抓住了我。

00:32:13.350 --> 00:32:15.350
<v President George H.w. Bush>Now, what's your question?
<v President George H.w. Bush>现在，你的问题是什么？

00:32:15.350 --> 00:32:27.570
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Now, this I remember. Because I just assumed I was going to be arrested. I knew I wasn't going to be shot. That hadn't happened yet, so that's cool. And I'm like, "This is it." And what happened next was amazing because nothing happened next.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>这个我记得。因为我只是假设我会被逮捕。我知道我不会被枪击。那还没发生，所以那很好。我想，“就是现在了。” 接下来发生的事情很惊人，因为接下来什么也没发生。

00:32:27.570 --> 00:32:52.270
<v Charles Monroe-kane>I didn't get arrested. He had this thing where he had his hands on the podium, and he kind of moved his hands. I don't know what signals they have, or whatever, but they didn't arrest me. And then the worst possible thing in the world happened to me. He ain't going to arrest me, and he's about to engage me. And I was like, "Oh, my god. The President of the United States is speaking to me right now, and he basically started asking me questions. And I was like, "Holy mackerel." I really almost passed out.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我没被逮捕。他把手放在讲台上，他移动了一下手。我不知道他们有什么信号，不管怎样，他们没逮捕我。然后世界上最糟糕的事情发生了。他没逮捕我，而且他打算和我互动。我想，“噢，天哪。美国总统正在跟我说话，他基本上开始问我问题了。我当时觉得，‘天哪。’ 我真的差点晕过去。”

00:32:52.270 --> 00:32:55.350
<v President George H.w. Bush>Who are you, and who are you accredited to?
<v President George H.w. Bush>你是谁？你受谁的委托？

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:56.050
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>My name is Charles Kane.
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>我的名字是查尔斯·凯恩。

00:32:56.050 --> 00:32:57.350
<v President George H.w. Bush>Yeah.
<v President George H.w. Bush>是啊。

00:32:57.350 --> 00:32:58.630
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>I'm from the United States.
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>我来自美国。

00:32:58.630 --> 00:32:59.560
<v President George H.w. Bush>Yeah.
<v President George H.w. Bush>是啊。

00:32:59.560 --> 00:33:05.540
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>I work with a magazine in the Netherlands. It's a youth magazine. And we want to know why we're not taken seriously. We're an environmental group.
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>我在荷兰的一本杂志工作。那是一本青年杂志。我们想知道为什么我们没被认真对待。我们是一个环保组织。

00:33:05.540 --> 00:33:14.340
<v President George H.w. Bush>Well, maybe you're rude. People don't take rude people seriously. And if you interrupt a press conference like this, I'm sure that people would say that's why we don't take you seriously.
<v President George H.w. Bush>嗯，也许你很粗鲁。人们不把粗鲁的人当回事。如果你像这样打断新闻发布会，我相信人们会说这就是我们不把你当回事的原因。

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:14.870
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>[INAUDIBLE]
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>[听不清]

00:33:14.870 --> 00:33:22.260
<v President George H.w. Bush>Sit down, and I will take a question from you when we get in the question and answer period. Right now, I would like to continue my statement with your permission.
<v President George H.w. Bush>坐下，等我们到了提问环节我会回答你的问题。现在，请允许我继续我的陈述。

00:33:22.260 --> 00:33:24.970
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>Thank you, Captain. Go ahead.
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>谢谢你，队长。请继续。

00:33:24.970 --> 00:33:31.560
<v President George H.w. Bush>Now, where were we? We were talking about economic recovery. [CHUCKLES]
<v President George H.w. Bush>现在，我们刚才聊到哪儿了？我们刚才在谈论经济复苏。 [轻笑]

00:33:31.560 --> 00:33:36.910
<v Ira Glass>So the President gives this little nervous laugh and sort of looks down, and then he goes on with his regular statement.
<v Ira Glass>所以总统发出这种神经质的笑声，然后向下看，接着继续他的常规陈述。

00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:37.510
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Oh my god.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>天哪。

00:33:37.510 --> 00:33:39.340
<v Ira Glass>And this continues for a few minutes.
<v Ira Glass>这种情况持续了几分钟。

00:33:39.340 --> 00:33:41.280
<v Charles Monroe-kane>So I'm sitting down, That's all. I don't remember.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>所以我坐下了，仅此而已。我不记得了。

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:42.200
<v Ira Glass>So you're sitting down.
<v Ira Glass>所以你坐下了。

00:33:42.200 --> 00:33:46.730
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Why didn't I keep yelling? What a wimp. I guess I was probably-- but he gave me a cue there.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>为什么我没继续大喊？真是个懦夫。我猜我可能——但他给了我一个暗示。

00:33:46.730 --> 00:33:56.300
<v Ira Glass>Well, what do you mean why-- What he was saying was perfectly reasonable. "I'll get to you during the question-- look, we're going to talk, and it's going to be during the question and answer period. I'm just reading my opening statement." How are you going to argue with that?
<v Ira Glass>好吧，你说的“为什么”是什么意思——他所说的是完全合理的。“我会等提问环节再找你——看，我们要聊的，这会在问答环节进行。我只是在读我的开场白。”你怎么能反驳这个？

00:33:56.300 --> 00:34:00.950
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Yeah, but it's a protest. I was supposed to be in there yelling at him. I was supposed to get arrested. I thought I was supposed to get arrested.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>是啊，但这可是抗议。我本应该在那儿对他大喊大叫的。我本应该被逮捕的。我以为我本应该被逮捕。

00:34:00.950 --> 00:34:05.090
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Oh my god, I so didn't want to be there after I didn't get arrested. I was so embarrassed. It was horrible.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>天哪，在没被逮捕后，我是多么不想待在那儿啊。我太尴尬了。那太可怕了。

00:34:05.090 --> 00:34:07.590
<v Ira Glass>Because from your point of view, the whole point was to get arrested.
<v Ira Glass>因为从你的角度看，整个目的就是为了被逮捕。

00:34:07.590 --> 00:34:07.880
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Yes.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>是的。

00:34:07.880 --> 00:34:09.050
<v Ira Glass>So now at this point, you've failed.
<v Ira Glass>所以现在在这一点上，你失败了。

00:34:09.050 --> 00:34:26.100
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Well, also, I not only failed at that point for not getting arrested, I also know I'm going to fail because he's going to ask a question to me later. Because I don't have anything to say. I mean, you've got to remember that. I don't have anything to say. I don't have a question. What do you mean, I have a question? Of course, I don't have a question.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>嗯，还有，我不但在那一刻因为没被逮捕而失败，我也知道我要失败了，因为他之后会问我一个问题。因为我没什么可说的。我的意思是，你得记住这一点。我没什么可说的。我没有问题。你说的“我有一个问题”是什么意思？当然，我没有问题。

00:34:26.100 --> 00:34:33.090
<v Ira Glass>So finally it comes to you. You're the third question. And basically, he answers two questions, and then he says this.
<v Ira Glass>所以最后轮到你了。你是第三个问题。基本上，他回答了两个问题，然后他说了这个。

00:34:33.090 --> 00:34:38.560
<v President George H.w. Bush>And that's what we're talking about. Now, let's go to this gentleman who's so agitated here.
<v President George H.w. Bush>而这就是我们正在谈论的。现在，让我们去问问这位非常激动的绅士。

00:34:38.560 --> 00:34:46.590
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>I just want to know why there's no new nuclear power plants in the United States being built, but you're proposing for Siemens to build them in Eastern Europe?
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>我只是想知道为什么美国没有在建新的核电站，而你却提议让西门子在东欧建造它们？

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:49.350
<v President George H.w. Bush>Oh, I'd like some more to be built.
<v President George H.w. Bush>噢，我希望多建一些。

00:34:49.350 --> 00:34:51.340
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>Why are they so unsafe in our country and so safe in their country?
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>为什么它们在我国不安全，而在他们国家却安全？

00:34:51.340 --> 00:34:53.010
<v President George H.w. Bush>Well, I don't think they're un--
<v President George H.w. Bush>嗯，我不认为它们不——

00:34:53.010 --> 00:34:54.330
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>Why it is only at the G7--
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>为什么只有在七国集团——

00:34:54.330 --> 00:35:19.610
<v President George H.w. Bush>You've asked your question, sir. Now let me try to answer it for you. I favor nuclear power. I believe that it can be safely used. I believe that it is environmentally sound. The debate here has been that we ought to try to help those areas that have nuclear facilities that might not have the latest technology and might not meet the same standards of safety that we use in our country. Thank you very much. Now, we'll go here.
<v President George H.w. Bush>你已经问过你的问题了，先生。现在让我试着为你回答它。我支持核能。我相信它可以被安全地利用。我相信它在环保方面是合理的。这里的辩论是我们应该尝试帮助那些拥有可能没有最新技术、可能不符合我们在我国使用的相同安全标准的核设施的地区。非常感谢。现在，我们到这里。

00:35:19.610 --> 00:35:20.200
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>Do you respect the flag?
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>你尊重国旗吗？

00:35:20.200 --> 00:35:21.240
<v Ira Glass>Then you said, "Do you respect the flag?"
<v Ira Glass>然后你说，“你尊重国旗吗？”

00:35:21.240 --> 00:35:22.940
<v President George H.w. Bush>You had your question.
<v President George H.w. Bush>你已经问过你的问题了。

00:35:22.940 --> 00:35:24.460
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Oh, now the media is yelling at me.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>噢，现在媒体在对我大喊大叫。

00:35:24.460 --> 00:35:24.940
<v Charles Monroe-kane>[CROWD YELLING]
<v Charles Monroe-kane>[人群叫喊声]

00:35:24.940 --> 00:35:27.360
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>It's all rhetoric. Come on, you guys, think about it.
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>那全是修辞。来吧，你们这些人，想想看。

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:28.930
<v Ira Glass>And then you say, "It's all rhetoric."
<v Ira Glass>然后你说，“那全是修辞。”

00:35:28.930 --> 00:35:30.390
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>Is the world going to be a better place in a year?
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>一年后世界会变得更美好吗？

00:35:30.390 --> 00:35:34.820
<v President George H.w. Bush>This is coming out of your time, gang, and we've got 20 minutes here.
<v President George H.w. Bush>这在浪费你的时间，伙计们，我们在这儿有20分钟。

00:35:34.820 --> 00:35:36.250
<v Media Member>This is a press conference, man.
<v Media Member>这是新闻发布会，伙计。

00:35:36.250 --> 00:35:39.090
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>Come on. This [UNINTELLIGIBLE] acting up at all.
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>来吧。这个 [听不清] 根本没起到什么作用。

00:35:39.090 --> 00:35:40.470
<v Sam Donaldson>Mr. President--
<v Sam Donaldson>总统先生——

00:35:40.470 --> 00:35:40.890
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>You guys are all part of this system too.
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>你们这些人也是这个系统的一部分。

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:42.210
<v Ira Glass>And then the president sort of laughs.
<v Ira Glass>然后总统笑了笑。

00:35:42.210 --> 00:35:43.740
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>Thanks a lot. Go ahead.
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>多谢。请继续。

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:45.850
<v Sam Donaldson>Much has been said here by you and others about the--
<v Sam Donaldson>你和其他人在这里说了关于——

00:35:45.850 --> 00:35:46.320
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>We've given up.
<v Charles Monroe-kane At Press Conference>我们放弃了。

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:50.800
<v Ira Glass>And then there's the voice of Sam Donaldson. And then now, it's back to normal.
<v Ira Glass>然后是萨姆·唐纳森的声音。然后现在，一切恢复正常了。

00:35:50.800 --> 00:36:09.780
<v Charles Monroe-kane>God, I can't believe I did that. That's embarrassing. It's so hot in here. I'm totally embarrassed by that. Oh my god. I mean, I'm not embarrassed about yelling at the President of the United States. I do not sit here right now and say, "Wow, I wish I wouldn't have done it." No way. I sit here right now and say, "I wish I would have done it down better."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>天哪，我不敢相信我竟然做了那样的事。那太尴尬了。这儿太热了。我对那感到非常尴尬。天哪。我的意思是，我不尴尬大喊美国总统这件事。我不坐在现在这里说，“哇，我希望我没那样做。”绝对没有。我坐在现在这里说，“我希望我当时做得更好。”

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:15.090
<v Ira Glass>And what would doing a better job have meant? What do you wish that you would have said?
<v Ira Glass>而做得更好意味着什么？你希望你当时会说什么？

00:36:15.090 --> 00:36:30.590
<v Charles Monroe-kane>I wish I would have said something that kind of hung in the air for a moment, that made everybody silent, that would keep him awake for one moment of his life, that just would make him think, "Wow, I do have some responsibility, and I have squandered that responsibility."
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我希望我说出一些会在那一刻悬在空气中、让每个人都沉默、能让他清醒哪怕一刻的话，那只会让他想，“哇，我确实有一些责任，而我浪费了那份责任。”

00:36:30.590 --> 00:36:32.980
<v Ira Glass>But what could that possibly be?
<v Ira Glass>但那可能是什么呢？

00:36:32.980 --> 00:36:38.980
<v Charles Monroe-kane>I don't know. Haven't you been touched by one statement in your life that's affected you greatly? I have.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我不知道。难道你的人生中没有被哪一句话深刻影响过吗？我有。

00:36:38.980 --> 00:36:40.230
<v Ira Glass>Yes, but it's--
<v Ira Glass>是的，但它是——

00:36:40.230 --> 00:36:40.810
<v Charles Monroe-kane>So I'm giving him credit.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>所以我正在给他信用。

00:36:40.810 --> 00:36:43.750
<v Ira Glass>It seems very unlikely in this setting.
<v Ira Glass>在这个环境中似乎非常不可能。

00:36:43.750 --> 00:36:50.700
<v Charles Monroe-kane>Now, in retrospect, it's extremely unlikely. It's probably a million to one, but you got to try. You got to try
<v Charles Monroe-kane>现在，回想起来，这极不可能。这可能是百万分之一的概率，但你得尝试。你得尝试。

00:36:50.700 --> 00:37:08.490
<v Ira Glass>I don't know. When I see the president answering questions at a news conference, I feel like what he's doing is-- he doesn't want to say anything that's going to get him in trouble. He doesn't want to say anything that's going to make his life more complicated and difficult. And it's not an environment conducive to learning.
<v Ira Glass>我不知道。当我看到总统在新闻发布会上回答问题时，我觉得他在做的是——他不想说任何会让他陷入麻烦的话。他不想说任何会让他的生活变得更复杂和困难的话。这不是一个有利于学习的环境。

00:37:08.490 --> 00:37:25.390
<v Charles Monroe-kane>What I should have said is-- when I think about it at 3 o'clock in the morning, it's not the-- it's in general what I should have said. I should have said something that would affect the man's life. And what's depressing for me that really makes me sad is that I still haven't come up with what I should have said. And that really makes me sad.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我本该说的是——当我在凌晨三点想到它时，这不是——这通常是我本该说的。我本该说些会影响那个人生活的话。真正让我感到沮丧、真正让我难过的是，我还没想出我本该说什么。这真的让我很难过。

00:37:25.390 --> 00:37:29.860
<v Ira Glass>But of course, you didn't come up with what you should have said because nothing could have been said--
<v Ira Glass>但当然，你没想出你本该说什么，因为什么也说不出来——

00:37:29.860 --> 00:37:30.870
<v Charles Monroe-kane>I won't accept that--
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我不接受这点——

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:35.820
<v Ira Glass>In that setting that was going to make the President of the United States rethink anything in particular.
<v Ira Glass>在那个环境下，能让美国总统反思任何特别的事情。

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:43.290
<v Charles Monroe-kane>I won't accept that. I should have found something that could have changed the world at that moment. I had my opportunity at that moment. I think I should have gone for it.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我不接受这点。我本该找到能在那一刻改变世界的东西。我在那一刻有机会。我觉得我本该去尝试。

00:37:43.290 --> 00:37:51.320
<v Ira Glass>Now, you're in your thirties now. Have you ever had this experience where somebody just yelled something at you, some punk kid, whatever, yelled at you?
<v Ira Glass>现在，你三十多岁了。你有过这样的经历吗，有人冲你大喊大叫，什么小混混，什么的，冲你大喊？

00:37:51.320 --> 00:37:59.440
<v Charles Monroe-kane>I did. I was at an introduction. I was introducing somebody who was about to give a speech. They heckled me. It was a really interesting experience because immediately what came back was me.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>我有过。我在一次介绍会上。我正在介绍一位即将发表演讲的人。他们嘲讽我。那是一次非常有趣的经历，因为立刻回击的是我自己。

00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:02.390
<v Ira Glass>Did it keep you up at night afterwards? Did it give you something to think about?
<v Ira Glass>那让你之后彻夜难眠吗？那给了你什么思考的东西吗？

00:38:02.390 --> 00:38:11.590
<v Charles Monroe-kane>That's an unfair-- of course, no, it didn't give me anything to think about. No, it didn't keep me up at night at all. It didn't-- yeah, I get your-- that's a good question. No, it didn't keep me up at all.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>那是个不公平的——当然，没有，它没给我任何思考的东西。没有，它根本没让我彻夜难眠。它没——是啊，我明白了你的意思——那是个好问题。没有，它根本没让我晚上睡不着。

00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:18.510
<v Ira Glass>And so do you think, thinking about George Bush and the possibility that he would be kept up at night a moment like this--
<v Ira Glass>所以你觉得，考虑到乔治·布什以及他可能会因为像这样的时刻而彻夜难眠的可能性——

00:38:18.510 --> 00:38:34.210
<v Charles Monroe-kane>No. No, OK, I understand where you're going. I respect where you're going. No, of course not. But I know this all along. Yeah, I know this all along. It's only what you wish would have happened. I hope-- I don't know. Maybe I'll read his memoirs, and he'll say, "You know--"
<v Charles Monroe-kane>不。不，好吧，我明白你的意思了。我尊重你的方向。不，当然不会。但我一直都知道这一点。是啊，我一直都知道这一点。那只是你希望发生的事情。我希望——我不知道。也许我会读他的回忆录，他会说，“你知道——”

00:38:34.210 --> 00:38:46.230
<v Charles Monroe-kane>OK, forget it. I won't even go there. I tried, man. It was a tough day, man. It was a tough day. I tried really. I did my best. My best wasn't very good at the time, but I tried my best.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>好吧，算了吧。我甚至不想去那儿。我尝试过了，伙计。那是个艰难的一天，伙计。那是个艰难的一天。我确实尽力了。我的全力当时并不是很好，但我尽力了。

00:38:46.230 --> 00:40:01.780
<v Ira Glass>Charles Monroe-Kane. He's a producer for the public radio program To The Best of Our Knowledge. Coming up, David Sedaris and When You Wish Upon A Pie. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>查尔斯·门罗-凯恩。他是公共广播节目《尽我们所知》的制作人。接下来是戴维·塞达里斯和《当你向派许愿》。一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:40:01.780 --> 00:40:25.700
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, What I Should've Said. Stories where people go back in time, rethink the moments that have vexed them. We have arrived at Act Three of our show. Act Three, A Can Of Worms. David Sedaris told this next story before a live audience.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，我们当然会选择一个主题，为您带来各种各样的关于该主题的故事。今天的节目，“我本该说的话”。人们回到过去，重新思考那些困扰他们的时刻的故事。我们已经进入节目的第三幕。第三幕，麻烦事。戴维·塞达里斯在一个现场观众面前讲述了这下一个故事。

00:40:25.700 --> 00:40:45.480
<v David Sedaris>Hugh wanted hamburgers. So he, his friend Anne, and I went to a place called The Apple Pan. This was in Los Angeles. I thought The Apple Pan would be a restaurant, but it was more like a diner. No tables, just stools arranged along a u-shaped counter.
<v David Sedaris>休想吃汉堡。所以他、他的朋友安和我去了叫“苹果锅”的地方。那是在洛杉矶。我以为“苹果锅”会是一家餐馆，但它更像个小饭店。没有桌子，只有沿着U型柜台排列的凳子。

00:40:45.480 --> 00:41:13.220
<v David Sedaris>We ordered our hamburgers from a man in a paper hat. And while waiting for them to arrive, Anne pulled out some pictures of her bull terrier. She's a professional photographer, and so they were portraits rather than snapshots. Here was the dog peeking out from behind a curtain. Here was the dog sitting human style in an easy chair, a paw resting on the paunch of his stomach.
<v David Sedaris>我们从一个戴纸帽子的男人那里点了汉堡。在等待它们到来时，安拿出了她牛头梗的照片。她是专业摄影师，所以它们是肖像照而不是快照。这里是狗从窗帘后面探出头来。这里是狗像人一样坐在一把扶手椅上，一只爪子放在肚子上。

00:41:13.220 --> 00:41:49.580
<v David Sedaris>When she's not taking pictures of her dog, Anne flies around the country on assignment from various magazines. A day earlier, she'd returned from Boston where she photographed the fire commissioner, whose last name is Bastardo. "That's bastard with an O on the end," she said. "Don't you think that's funny?" Hugh told her about some neighbors in Normandy whose last name translates to hot ass. But unless you speak French, it's hard to get the joke.
<v David Sedaris>当她不拍她的狗时，安会受各种杂志的指派飞往全国各地。前一天，她从波士顿回来，在那里她拍摄了消防专员，他的姓是巴斯塔多。“那是混蛋（bastard）加个O在后面，”她说。“你不觉得这很有趣吗？” 休告诉她关于诺曼底的一些邻居，他们的姓翻译过来是“热屁股”（hot ass）。但除非你会法语，否则很难理解这个笑话。

00:41:49.580 --> 00:41:58.810
<v David Sedaris>"Is that hyphenated," Anne asked. "I mean, did Miss Hot marry Mr. Ass, or is it all one word?"
<v David Sedaris>“那是连字符的吗？”安问。“我的意思是，是小小姐嫁给了屁股先生，还是它是一个词？”

00:41:58.810 --> 00:42:31.340
<v David Sedaris>I thought the conversation would rest here for a while, and so I prepared myself to contribute, wary of how easy it is to fall into a game of one-upsmanship. If you knew a Candy Dick, the other person is bound to have known a Harry Dick or a Dick Eater. I'd recently learned of the race car driver Dick Trickle. But for the time being, we were operating on a higher plane.
<v David Sedaris>我以为谈话会在这里停留一会儿，所以我准备参与，小心翼翼地想避免陷入某种攀比游戏。如果你认识一个Candy Dick（姓氏），对方注定认识一个Harry Dick或者Dick Eater。我最近听说过赛车手迪克·特里克尔（Dick Trickle）。但目前，我们在更高的平面上运作。

00:42:31.340 --> 00:43:03.930
<v David Sedaris>Anne's last name is Fishbein, and she hates it. She and Hugh met in college, and when our hamburgers arrived, they reminisced about some of the people they had gone to school with. "What was that guy's name? I think he was in the art department. Mike maybe, or Mark? He used to go out with Karen, I think her name was. Or Kimberly. You know who I mean?" Talk like this can go on for a while. And while you do have to accept it, you don't have to actually pay attention.
<v David Sedaris>安的姓是费什拜因，她讨厌它。她和休在大学里认识，当我们的汉堡上来时，他们回忆起了一些他们一起上过学的同学。“那家伙叫什么名字来着？我觉得他在艺术系。迈克也许，还是马克？他曾经和凯伦约会过，我想她叫那个名字。还是金伯利。你知道我说的是谁吗？” 这样的对话可以持续一会儿。虽然你确实得接受它，但你不需要真正去注意它。

00:43:03.930 --> 00:43:41.450
<v David Sedaris>I stared straight ahead, watching a broken-nosed cook top a hamburger with cheese. And then I turned slightly to my left and began listening to the two men seated on the other side of me. The man beside me wore a t-shirt endorsing the state of Florida. And as if the weather were completely different on the other side of the ketchup bottle, the man beside him wore a thick wool sweater and heavy corduroy pants. A coat rested in his lap, and before him on the counter sat a newspaper and an empty cup of coffee.
<v David Sedaris>我盯着前方，看着一个塌鼻子的厨师在汉堡上加芝士。然后我稍微向左转，开始听坐在我另一边的两个男人说话。坐在我旁边的男人穿着一件支持佛罗里达州的T恤。好像番茄酱瓶子另一边的天气完全不同似的，他旁边的男人穿着一件厚羊毛衫和粗灯芯绒裤。外套放在他的膝盖上，在他面前的柜台上放着一份报纸和一杯喝光的咖啡。

00:43:41.450 --> 00:44:06.180
<v David Sedaris>"Did you read about those worms," he asked? He was referring to the can of nematodes, tiny worms recently discovered on the Texas plains. They'd been sent up with the doomed space shuttle and had somehow managed to survive the explosion, the cause of which was still a mystery. The man in the sweater massaged his chin and stared into space.
<v David Sedaris>“你读过关于那些虫子的报道吗？”他问。他指的是线虫罐头，最近在德克萨斯平原发现的微小蠕虫。它们曾随注定毁灭的航天飞机被送上天，并以某种方式在爆炸中幸存下来，爆炸的原因仍然是个谜。穿羊毛衫的男人按摩着下巴，盯着虚空。

00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:29.760
<v David Sedaris>"I've been thinking we could solve this problem in no time," he said, "if only, if only we could get the damn things to talk." It sounded crazy, but I remember thinking the exact same thing about the Akita in the O.J. Simpson case.
<v David Sedaris>“我一直在想，我们可以迅速解决这个问题，”他说，“只要，只要我们能让这些该死的东西说话。” 这听起来很疯狂，但我记得在O.J.辛普森案中的秋田犬身上想过同样的事情。

00:44:29.760 --> 00:44:49.010
<v David Sedaris>"Put it on the stand. Let's hear what it's got to say." It was one of those ideas that just for a split second seemed entirely logical, the one solution that nobody had thought of. The man in the t-shirt considered the possibility.
<v David Sedaris>“把它放在证人席上。让我们听听它有什么要说的。” 这是那种想法，在那一瞬间看起来完全合理，这是没人想到的唯一解决方案。穿T恤的男人考虑了这种可能性。

00:44:49.010 --> 00:44:59.640
<v David Sedaris>"Well," he said, "even if the worms could talk, it wouldn't do much good. They was in that can, remember?"
<v David Sedaris>“嗯，”他说，“即使蠕虫会说话，也没什么用。它们在那个罐子里，记得吗？”

00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:02.060
<v David Sedaris>"I guess you're right."
<v David Sedaris>“我想你是对的。”

00:45:02.060 --> 00:45:27.900
<v David Sedaris>The men stood to pay their bills. And before they reached the door, their stools were taken by two people who did not know one another. One was a man dressed in a fine suit, and the other, a young woman who sat down and immediately started reading what looked to be a script. Over on my right, Hugh had decided that rather than Karen or Kimberly, the classmate had been named Katherine.
<v David Sedaris>男人们站起来去结账。在他们到达门口之前，他们的凳子被两个互不认识的人占领了。一个是身穿名牌西装的男人，另一个是年轻女子，她坐下后立刻开始阅读看起来像是剧本的东西。在我的右边，休已经决定，比起凯伦或金伯利，那个同学叫凯瑟琳。

00:45:27.900 --> 00:45:41.430
<v David Sedaris>While I'd been listening to my neighbors, he had ordered me a piece of pie. And as I picked up my fork, Anne told me that I was supposed to eat it backwards, starting with the outer crust and working my way inward.
<v David Sedaris>当我在听邻居谈话时，他已经帮我点了一块派。当我拿起叉子时，安告诉我我应该从反面吃，从外壳开始，然后向内吃。

00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:51.390
<v David Sedaris>"Your last bite should be the point, and you're supposed to make a wish on it," she said. "Hasn't anyone ever told you that?"
<v David Sedaris>“你的最后一口应该是尖儿，你应该对着它许个愿，”她说。“从来没人告诉过你吗？”

00:45:51.390 --> 00:45:54.200
<v David Sedaris>"Come again?"
<v David Sedaris>“再说一遍？”

00:45:54.200 --> 00:46:08.160
<v David Sedaris>Anne looked at me the way you might at someone who regularly tosses money into the fire. The senselessness, the waste.
<v David Sedaris>安看着我的眼神，就像看一个经常把钱扔进火里的人一样。那种愚蠢，那种浪费。

00:46:08.160 --> 00:46:13.940
<v David Sedaris>"Well, better late than never," she said. And she repositioned my plate.
<v David Sedaris>“嗯，迟做总比不做好，”她说。然后她重新摆放了我的盘子。

00:46:13.940 --> 00:47:05.210
<v David Sedaris>As she and Hugh resumed their conversation, I thought of all the pie I had eaten during the course of my life, and I wondered how different things might be if only I had wished upon the points. To begin with, I would not be seated at The Apple Pan. That much was certain. Had I gotten my wish at the age of eight, I would still be rounding up mummies in Egypt, luring them from their tombs and trapping them in heavy iron cages. All subsequent wishes would have been based upon the life I had already established. A new set of boots. A finer whip. Greater command of the mummy language.
<v David Sedaris>当她和休恢复谈话时，我想起了我一生中吃过的所有派，我好奇如果我早些时候对着尖儿许愿，事情会有什么不同。首先，我不会坐在“苹果锅”。那是肯定的。如果我在八岁时就许了愿，我可能还在埃及搜寻木乃伊，把它们引出坟墓并把它们困在沉重的铁笼里。所有随后的愿望都将建立在我已经建立的生活基础上。一套新靴子。一条更好的鞭子。对木乃伊语言更好的掌握。

00:47:05.210 --> 00:47:35.500
<v David Sedaris>That's the problem with wishes. They ensnare you. In fairy tales, they're nothing but trouble, magnifying the greed and vanity of the person for whom they are granted. One's best bet, and the moral to all those stories, is to be unselfish and make your wish for the benefit of others, trusting that their happiness will make you happy as well. That'll be the day.
<v David Sedaris>这就是愿望的问题所在。它们会困住你。在童话故事中，它们除了麻烦什么都不是，只会放大被授予愿望的人的贪婪和虚荣。一个人最好的赌注，也是所有这些故事的道德所在，是无私并为他人的利益许愿，相信他们的幸福也会让你幸福。那才怪呢。

00:47:35.500 --> 00:48:14.300
<v David Sedaris>Since we'd entered, The Apple Pan had grown progressively busier. All the seats were now taken. And people leaned against the wall, their eyes moving from stool to stool, determining which customers should pay up and get out. Looking around, I saw that we were the likeliest candidates. The man in the hat had removed our hamburger wrappers, and all that remained was a single plate supporting the tip of my pie. I wished that the people against the wall would stop staring at us, and then quickly, but not quick enough, I tried to take it back.
<v David Sedaris>自从我们进来以后，“苹果锅”已经变得越来越忙碌。所有的座位现在都满了。人们靠在墙上，眼睛在凳子之间移动，确定哪些顾客应该付账离开。环顾四周，我看到我们是最可能的候选人。那个戴帽子的男人已经收走了我们的汉堡包装纸，剩下的只有支持我派尖儿的一个盘子。我希望靠在墙上的人别再盯着我们看了，然后很快，但不够快，我试图收回那个愿望。

00:48:14.300 --> 00:49:03.310
<v David Sedaris>"I guess we should get going," Hugh said, and he and Anne pulled out their wallets. There was a little struggle over who would pay. "It's my treat." "No, it's mine." But I stayed out of it, thinking instead of what might have been had I not wasted my wish. A laboratory filled with sensitive equipment. Men in white coats trembling with hope and wonder as they leaned forward, catching the sound of one small voice. "Come to think of it," the worm says, "I do remember seeing something suspicious."
<v David Sedaris>“我想我们该走了，”休说，他和安掏出了钱包。关于谁来买单有一点小挣扎。“我请客。” “不，我请。” 但我没参与，而是想着如果我没浪费我的愿望，可能是什么样子。一个装满敏感设备的实验室。穿白大褂的男人颤抖着希望和惊奇，向前探着身子，捕捉着一个小声音。“说起来，”蠕虫说，“我确实记得看到了一些可疑的东西。”

00:49:03.310 --> 00:49:10.840
<v Ira Glass>David Sedaris. This story appears in his most recent book, Dress Your Family in Corduroy and Denim.
<v Ira Glass>戴维·塞达里斯。这个故事出现在他最近的一本书《把你的家人打扮成灯芯绒和丹宁布》中。

00:49:10.840 --> 00:49:44.530
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "THE GLOW WORM" BY THE MILLS BROTHERS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 米尔斯兄弟的《发光虫》]

00:49:44.530 --> 00:50:02.290
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Life Sentence. In 1989, a politician named Mike Miller, president of the Maryland State Senate, the kind of political veteran who does not slip up, made a comment to a reporter-- just a few sentences off the cuff-- that many political observers say put the kibosh on his chances to become governor.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，终身监禁。1989年，一位名叫迈克·米勒的政治家，马里兰州参议院院长，那种不会犯错的政坛老手，对一名记者发表了评论——只是随口说的几句话——许多政治观察家说这摧毁了他成为州长的机会。

00:50:02.290 --> 00:50:25.400
<v Ira Glass>The day this happened actually happened to be the day of a big political coming-out party for Miller, his first statewide fundraiser, to be held over in Baltimore. Miller comes from a district near DC. He'd sold over 2,000 tickets. Both US senators would be there, and the governor, and the mayor of Baltimore. But also, the same day, one of his law partners died at a desk in their law offices. So a lot was on his mind.
<v Ira Glass>这件事发生的当天正好是米勒的一场大型政治出场派对的日子，他第一次全州范围的筹款活动，将在巴尔的摩举行。米勒来自华盛顿特区附近的一个选区。他卖出了超过2000张票。两位美国参议员都会出席，还有州长，以及巴尔的摩市长。但同样在这一天，他的一位法律合伙人在他们的律师事务所办公桌前去世了。所以他心里想的事很多。

00:50:25.400 --> 00:50:31.250
<v Ira Glass>And with all this happening, he gets a call from a TV reporter who was doing a story about a local gun control issue.
<v Ira Glass>而就在所有这些事情发生的时候，他接到了一个正在做关于当地枪支管制问题的故事的电视记者的电话。

00:50:31.250 --> 00:51:01.130
<v Mike Miller>She came to my office, and I was sitting there very despondent about the law partner who had been found dead sitting just a few hours before in his chair in his office. And she comes in and starts stalking to me-- no mic check or no white-on-white check or anything like that. She said to me, "Why are you going to Baltimore?" In other words, you're not from this area in Baltimore, you're from Washington. Why are you going to Baltimore?
<v Mike Miller>她来到我的办公室，我当时因为那位合伙人被发现死在办公室椅子上几个小时而非常沮丧。她进来开始跟我说话——没有麦克风测试，也没有白色衬衫测试之类的。她对我说，“你为什么要去巴尔的摩？” 换句话说，你不是巴尔的摩这个区域的人，你是华盛顿的人。你为什么要去巴尔的摩？

00:51:01.130 --> 00:51:32.890
<v Mike Miller>And I used some profanity-laced language about the city, defaming the city, which I love. But I was just using it to describe the need of the city in terms of jobs, in terms of economic development, in terms of education. But I said it in barnyard phraseology that-- which is sort of my standard nomenclature when I'm not in public life. I mean, I grew up in the country, you know?
<v Mike Miller>我用了一些满是粗口的语言谈论这个城市，诋毁这个我所爱的城市。但我只是用它来描述这个城市在工作、经济发展、教育方面的需求。但我用的是一种谷仓式的措辞——这在我不处于公共生活时是我的标准命名法。我的意思是，我在乡下长大，你知道吧？

00:51:32.890 --> 00:51:39.560
<v Ira Glass>I don't mean to embarrass you for this thing. It happened 15 years ago. But let me just read the exact quote so people listening on the radio will know what we're talking about.
<v Ira Glass>我不想因为这件事让你难堪。这发生在15年前。但让我读出那句确切的引语，这样收听广播的人就会知道我们在谈论什么。

00:51:39.560 --> 00:51:40.280
<v Mike Miller>OK.
<v Mike Miller>好的。

00:51:40.280 --> 00:52:10.930
<v Ira Glass>You were asked why were you holding a fundraiser in Baltimore, and you said, "It helps educate my constituents as to why Baltimore needs the economic help. I mean, Baltimore is a goddamn ghetto. It's worse than inner-city Washington, DC. It is [BLEEP]." They beeped out the words like we're doing. And then you say, on tape, apparently, "I hope you're not going to play this on tape." And you laughed, and then you said, "I mean, it's a war zone. I mean, it's crack. I mean, it's dime bags of PCP. 1/4 of every kid is not in school each day. 50% of the kids that start out in school don't graduate. So looking at things from a statewide perspective, we really have to do things to help."
<v Ira Glass>有人问你为什么在巴尔的摩举行筹款活动，你说，“这有助于教育我的选民为什么巴尔的摩需要经济帮助。我的意思是，巴尔的摩是个该死的贫民窟。它比华盛顿市中心还糟。它是个 [消音]。” 他们消音了这些词，就像我们正在做的一样。然后你在录音带上说，显然，“我希望你不打算把这个放在带子上录下来。” 然后你笑了，接着说，“我的意思是，它是个战区。我的意思是，它到处是快克毒品。我的意思是，它是一角钱一包的PCP（苯环利定）。每四个孩子里就有一个每天没去上学。学校里开始的孩子有50%没能毕业。所以从全州的视角来看，我们真的必须采取行动来帮助。”

00:52:10.930 --> 00:52:13.270
<v Mike Miller>Right. That's what I said.
<v Mike Miller>没错。我就是那么说的。

00:52:13.270 --> 00:52:15.690
<v Ira Glass>But you didn't know they were actually rolling film?
<v Ira Glass>但你当时不知道他们真的在开机录制？

00:52:15.690 --> 00:52:35.930
<v Mike Miller>No, absolutely not. But I didn't know that I had said anything wrong. I mean, I really didn't realize that I'd said anything wrong. I had no idea I'd said anything wrong. Nobody told me I was on television at the time. And so later on, she does her interview, and everything is fine. Doesn't mention anything, we don't follow up with it or anything like that.
<v Mike Miller>不，绝对不知道。但我不知道我说了什么错话。我的意思是，我真的没意识到我说了什么错话。我完全不知道我说了什么错话。当时没人告诉我我在电视上。所以后来，她做了她的采访，一切都很好。没提到任何事，我们也没跟进或者类似的事情。

00:52:35.930 --> 00:52:53.150
<v Mike Miller>And then I get a call later on. Apparently, she'd got the tape, and she'd run to the Mayor of Baltimore, "Let me show you this." And she put it on the Baltimore stations, and then put it on the Washington stations. And they would make the announcement, "Be careful, have your children leave the room before you see this."
<v Mike Miller>然后我后来接到了一个电话。显然，她拿到了带子，跑去找巴尔的摩市长，“让我给你看这个。” 她把它放在巴尔的摩的电视台播，然后又放在华盛顿的电视台播。他们会发布公告，“小心点，在你看到这个之前让你的孩子离开房间。”

00:52:53.150 --> 00:52:53.920
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:52:53.920 --> 00:53:05.880
<v Mike Miller>It was very traumatic. Because like I say, the people showed up at the fundraiser, and the mayor of Baltimore was there and the governor. And they couldn't believe that I'd said what I said.
<v Mike Miller>那非常痛苦。因为就像我说的，人们出现在筹款活动上，巴尔的摩市长在那儿，州长也在那儿。他们无法相信我说了我所说的话。

00:53:05.880 --> 00:53:12.870
<v Ira Glass>So when something like this happens, is this just like a bomb going off? Does everything turn over in a minute?
<v Ira Glass>所以当这种事发生时，这就像炸弹爆炸了吗？一切在瞬间翻转了吗？

00:53:12.870 --> 00:53:36.870
<v Mike Miller>Yes, very much so. I couldn't sleep. I left the hotel at 4 o'clock in the morning. I left my wife at the hotel. I couldn't sleep. I just had to get out of the city. I said, "Look, I'm leaving." And the doorman, he said, "Senator?" He says, "I don't understand this." He says, "You've always been great to Baltimore. I don't understand this." He says, "We'll figure a way out of this." He says, "You and I will figure a way out of this." This is the doorman at the hotel.
<v Mike Miller>是的，非常如此。我无法入睡。我在凌晨4点离开了酒店。我把妻子留在了酒店。我睡不着。我只是得离开那个城市。我说，“看，我要走了。” 那个门房，他说，“参议员？” 他说，“我不明白这点。” 他说，“你对巴尔的摩一直很好。我不明白这点。” 他说，“我们会想出办法解决这个的。” 他说，“你和我会想出办法解决这个的。” 这是酒店的门房。

00:53:36.870 --> 00:53:37.600
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:53:37.600 --> 00:53:40.050
<v Mike Miller>And I said, "I appreciate it. Thank you very much." And anyway--
<v Mike Miller>我说，“我很感激。非常感谢。” 总之——

00:53:40.050 --> 00:53:43.010
<v Ira Glass>Man, I've got to say, that's a bad number when--
<v Ira Glass>伙计，我得说，那是个糟糕的数字，当——

00:53:43.010 --> 00:53:43.940
<v Mike Miller>He was a great guy, he was a great guy.
<v Mike Miller>他是个很棒的家伙，他是个很棒的家伙。

00:53:43.940 --> 00:53:46.630
<v Ira Glass>When the doorman of the hotel is consoling you.
<v Ira Glass>当酒店的门房在安慰你时。

00:53:46.630 --> 00:53:49.460
<v Mike Miller>The doorman of the hotel. He said, "Look, Senator, I'll help you. We'll figure a way--"
<v Mike Miller>酒店的门房。他说，“看，参议员，我会帮你的。我们会想出办法——”

00:53:49.460 --> 00:53:50.970
<v Ira Glass>This is at 4:00 in the morning?
<v Ira Glass>这是在凌晨4点？

00:53:50.970 --> 00:53:58.480
<v Mike Miller>Yes. So the next night, I'm on all the TV channels, 2, 11, 13. And anyway--
<v Mike Miller>是的。所以第二天晚上，我出现在所有电视频道上，2、11、13。总之——

00:53:58.480 --> 00:54:00.630
<v Ira Glass>You mean going back and giving your side of it?
<v Ira Glass>你的意思是回去并给出你的看法？

00:54:00.630 --> 00:54:21.700
<v Mike Miller>I went back in the city. I said, "Look, let me just explain this as best I can." And I explained it sort of the way I did to you. And the doorman goes-- I come back to the doorman because my wife had forgot her hat-- and he goes, "Senator, Senator," he said, "I knew you could do it, I knew you could do it." He said, "That country boy stuff gets them every time."
<v Mike Miller>我回到了城里。我说，“看，让我尽可能解释一下。” 我解释的方式跟我对你解释的方式差不多。然后门房走过来——我回来找门房是因为我妻子忘了她的帽子——他走过来，“参议员，参议员，”他说，“我知道你能行的，我知道你能行的。” 他说，“那种乡村小子的那套总是很管用。”

00:54:21.700 --> 00:54:24.940
<v Mike Miller>[LAUGHING]
<v Mike Miller>[笑声]

00:54:24.940 --> 00:54:35.460
<v Mike Miller>So anyway, it was very traumatic for me. And like I say, a lot of people took offense that I used the word ghetto for example. And at the time, I didn't realize the connotation.
<v Mike Miller>总之，这对我来说非常痛苦。就像我说的，很多人对我用了“贫民窟”这个词感到冒犯。当时，我没意识到它的内涵。

00:54:35.460 --> 00:54:44.900
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Now, this happened on a night of an exploratory fundraiser for statewide office. What effect did this have on your career when you look back?
<v Ira Glass>是啊。现在，这发生在你进行探索性全州办公室筹款活动的晚上。当你回顾过去时，这对你的职业生涯有什么影响？

00:54:44.900 --> 00:55:08.270
<v Mike Miller>If I were going to run for statewide office, it would certainly have set me back four to eight years. My adviser said, "If you had just taken that theme and expanded it and say what you really meant, you could have turned it into a positive." And I said, "Look, I don't really feel like doing that much explaining, and I don't want my mother to have to hear this statement again."
<v Mike Miller>如果我要竞选全州办公室，那肯定会让我推迟四到八年。我的顾问说，“如果你当初采取那种主题并扩展它，说出你真正想说的，你本可以把它变成一个积极的东西。” 我说，“看，我真的不想花那么多时间去解释，而且我不希望我母亲被迫再听一次这个声明。”

00:55:08.270 --> 00:55:19.840
<v Mike Miller>Because wherever I campaign, it's going to be on TV. It's going to be on sound bites and TV ads and wherever, and I really don't want to see that again on television or in print.
<v Mike Miller>因为无论我在哪里竞选，它都会出现在电视上。它会出现在音段和电视广告以及任何地方，我真的不想再在电视上或报纸上看到那个了。

00:55:19.840 --> 00:55:23.780
<v Ira Glass>This must have been one of those moments you ended up thinking about a lot afterwards, right?
<v Ira Glass>这一定是那些你后来反复思考的时刻之一，对吧？

00:55:23.780 --> 00:55:25.410
<v Mike Miller>Oh, yeah. I mean, I just--
<v Mike Miller>噢，是啊。我的意思是，我只是——

00:55:25.410 --> 00:55:30.570
<v Ira Glass>And what would you think? When you replay it in your head, what would you think about it?
<v Ira Glass>你会想什么？当你在脑海中重演它时，你会怎么想它？

00:55:30.570 --> 00:55:50.770
<v Mike Miller>Well, as a politician, they give you these things, how you're supposed to deal with reporters and the press. And what you're supposed to say, and you're always on record. But I've never been able to follow that guideline for myself. I just-- People appreciate your candidness, but at the same time, it gets you in a lot of trouble.
<v Mike Miller>嗯，作为一个政治家，他们会给你这些东西，关于你应该如何应对记者和媒体。以及你应该说什么，你总是处于记录中。但我从来没能为自己遵循那个准则。我只是——人们赞赏你的坦率，但同时，这会让你陷入很多麻烦。

00:55:50.770 --> 00:55:55.720
<v Ira Glass>Did you find that you were more careful in what you were saying afterwards?
<v Ira Glass>你发现之后说话更谨慎了吗？

00:55:55.720 --> 00:56:00.930
<v Mike Miller>No. But I did find that there were certain reporters I wouldn't talk to anymore.
<v Mike Miller>没有。但我发现有些记者我不再和他们说话了。

00:56:00.930 --> 00:56:08.620
<v Mike Miller>[LAUGHING]
<v Mike Miller>[笑声]

00:56:08.620 --> 00:56:33.510
<v Ira Glass>And I saw that a couple years ago, in 2001-- that's 12 years after this happened-- the Baltimore papers did a big profile of you, a very flattering profile summarizing your legislative career. And this is one of the things that they mentioned. Not in a big way, but just as one of the things that had happened in your career. Is it dispiriting feeling like you're just never going to shake this, you know what I mean? When you retire, it's going to be there? In your obit, they're going to mention this in passing?
<v Ira Glass>我看到几年前，2001年——那是在这事发生12年后——巴尔的摩报纸对你做了一个大的简介，一个非常恭维的简介，总结了你的立法生涯。这是他们提到的事情之一。不是以很大的方式，只是作为你职业生涯中发生的事件之一。感觉你永远无法摆脱这个，这种感觉令人沮丧吗，你知道我的意思吗？当你退休时，它也会在那里？在你的讣告里，他们会顺带提及这个？

00:56:33.510 --> 00:56:50.680
<v Mike Miller>It's going to be in my obituary. It's going to be in my obituary. There's a lot of things. And unfortunately, as somebody said, "History is lies agreed upon." Well, some of it's the truth, and you just have to take the good with the bad.
<v Mike Miller>它会出现在我的讣告里。它会出现在我的讣告里。有很多事情。不幸的是，正如某人所说，“历史是达成的共识的谎言。” 嗯，其中一些是真相，你只能取其精华去其糟粕。

00:56:50.680 --> 00:57:28.190
<v Ira Glass>Mike Miller. He never did run for governor or for any other statewide office. He's a state senator, and now the longest sitting president of the Maryland State Senate in that body's history.
<v Ira Glass>迈克·米勒。他确实从未竞选过州长或任何其他全州办公室。他是一名州参议员，现在是马里兰州参议院历史上任职时间最长的院长。

00:57:28.190 --> 00:57:53.500
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Starlee Kine and myself with Alex Blumberg, Diane Cook, Jane Feltes, and Sarah Koenig. Our senior producer is Julie Snyder. Elizabeth Meister runs our website. Production help from Todd Bachmann and Kelsey Dilts. Special thanks today to Luke Burbank and William Eville. You can download audio of our program at audible.com/thisamericanlife, where they have public radio programs, best-selling books, even The New York Times, all at audible.com. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我们的节目今天由斯塔莉·凯恩和我自己制作，还有亚历克斯·布卢姆伯格、黛安·库克、简·费尔特斯和萨拉·科尼格。我们的高级制作人是朱莉·斯奈德。伊丽莎白·迈斯特负责我们的网站。托德·巴赫曼和凯尔西·迪尔茨提供了制作帮助。今天特别感谢卢克·伯班克和威廉·埃维尔。您可以在audible.com/thisamericanlife下载我们节目的音频，那里有公共广播节目、畅销书，甚至《纽约时报》，都在audible.com。 《美国生活》由国际公共广播电台发行。

00:57:53.500 --> 00:58:12.450
<v Ira Glass>[FUNDING CREDITS]
<v Ira Glass>[资金鸣谢]

00:58:12.450 --> 00:58:23.190
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ management oversight for our program by Mr. Torey Malatia, who has a message for you, a very important message. And he wants you to listen very, very carefully. Are you ready?
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ管理层对我们节目的监督由托里·马拉蒂亚先生负责，他有一个信息给你，一个非常重要的信息。他希望你非常、非常仔细地倾听。你准备好了吗？

00:58:23.190 --> 00:58:29.560
<v Charles Monroe-kane>The homeless in the trees are mourning your economic decisions. Repent, dear King, or go to hell.
<v Charles Monroe-kane>树上的无家可归者在哀悼你的经济决策。忏悔吧，亲爱的国王，否则下地狱吧。

00:58:29.560 --> 00:58:54.000
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带回更多《美国生活》的故事。

00:58:54.000 --> 00:58:59.000
<v Announcer>PRI. Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>PRI。国际公共广播电台。
